3330187803	Jiquitaia	Rua Coronel Oscar Porto 808	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus	http://www.jiquitaia.com.br	Nessun premio	Brazilian	3.0	$	f	f	If you're looking for typical homemade Brazilian cuisine prepared with quality ingredients, then this is the perfect place! Jiquitaia occupies a charming 1950s house surrounded by greenery, with a large, elegantly decorated dining room, where a plethora of wood and beautiful photos on the walls provide the decorative backdrop. Here, chef Marcelo Corrêa Bastos conjures up comforting traditional cuisine that feeds body and soul. During the working week, the playful “executive” menu is complemented by a concise menu featuring delicacies such as “coxinhas de frango caipira” (deep-fried dough filled with chicken), fish “moqueca” with shrimp, and grilled fish of the day served with a tasty pirão porridge. It's also worth noting that Jiquitaia also has a superb array of cachaças from across Brazil which have been carefully selected by the owner.	naldinadia	\N	\N
3332276433	Vista	Avenida Pedro Álvares Cabral 1301	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Wheelchair access	https://vistarestaurante.vistasaopaulo.com.br/	Nessun premio	Brazilian	3.0	$$	t	f	Occupying the top floor of the MAC USP (Museu de Arte Contemporânea da Universidade de São Paulo), this restaurant boasts spectacular views of Ibirapuera and a 360° panorama of the city. The kitchen is run by chef Pedro Oliveira, who offers guests a traditional Brazilian dining experience featuring typical ingredients from the country’s five regions, which he prepares in a harmonious and balanced way and is showcased on an “executive”-style format at lunchtime. On Saturdays, pumpkin shrimp with rice and sweet potato chips is the highlight, with crispy pururuca pork making an appearance on Sundays. The star of the show on the dessert menu is Vista’s own irresistible version of the traditional Portuguese dish “toucinho do Céu”, made with pumpkin, egg yolk and almonds. A special menu of starters and appetisers, plus an impressive selection of cocktails, is available on the terrace.	moldovanmelly	\N	\N
3338722054	Casa Rios	Rua Itapura	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://www.casariosrestaurante.com.br/	Nessun premio	Brazilian, Contemporary	3.0	$$	f	f	Chef Rodrigo Aguiar, originally from Rio de Janeiro, combined his skills with owner Giovanna Perrone to realise her dream here. This charming restaurant features an open kitchen where guests can view the wood oven and churrasco grill, both of which exude the smoky flavours for which Casa Rios is famous. If you sit at the bar counter, you can watch the preparation process in full flow as well as appreciate the aromas emanating from the kitchen. The cuisine here is based around typical Brazilian cooking with a focus on the south-east of the country, using ingredients from small-scale producers and a constant nod to seasonality. Whether you opt for the tasting menu or à la carte, make sure you try the wood oven-baked pizzeta of merguez sausage made with cornbread – an absolute must!	farinellifarah	\N	\N
3334963308	Charco	Rua Peixoto Gomide 1492	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Terrace,Wheelchair access		Nessun premio	Brazilian, Grills	5.0	$$	t	t	In this restaurant inspired by the typical cuisine of the Rio Grande do Sul region, chef Tuca Mezzomo leads his team alongside Nathalia Gonçalves – masters in the art of preparing grilled and flame-cooked dishes with a continual focus on the best and freshest ingredients. Dishes that you won’t want to miss include the toasted mushrooms with smoked pumpkin and parmesan, and the succulent grilled Wagyu short rib, served with a wild tomato salad and a Bordelaise sauce. In addition, the restaurant also produces its own delicious sausages, butter and fresh goat’s cheese. The backdrop to the dining experience is a rustic, welcoming mix of exposed brickwork, wooden tables and subdued lighting that adds to the pleasant ambience.	fischerbogdan	\N	\N
3333344470	NOTIÊ	Rua Formosa 157	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://espacopriceless.com.br/	Nessun premio	Brazilian, Modern Cuisine	5.0	$$$	f	t	Although only recently opened, this restaurant is already being talked about by the city’s foodie community. Located on the rooftop of the Shopping Light building, its design comes as a pleasant surprise as soon as you walk through the door thanks to its decor of yellow tones, glass-fronted kitchen enabling guests to watch the chefs hard at work and, first and foremost, the 18m panel on the ceiling depicting images of nature. At the helm in the kitchen is Paraíba-born chef Onildo Rocha who impresses with his use of typical Brazilian ingredients and combinations that result in intense and striking dishes. His recipes are based around his journeys to different Brazilian regions every season, and are showcased on 5-, 7- and 11-course tasting menus. The name of the restaurant is a fusion between one of the country’s most colourful birds, the tanager (tiê in Portuguese) and the Portuguese word for night (noite).	landirobert	\N	\N
3334887899	A Casa do Porco	Rua Araujo 124	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Terrace	https://acasadoporco.com.br/	Nessun premio	Brazilian, Pork	3.0	$$	t	t	If you’re keen to try pork that you won’t find anywhere else, then this (as the name would suggest!) is the perfect place. Here, chefs Jefferson Rueda and Janaína Torres, who are responsible for rearing their pigs and for growing most of the vegetables used in their restaurant, have rekindled and revisited the flavours from their childhood through dishes showcasing sophisticated technique. In this informal and relaxed space, with an open kitchen and a small grocery store, their cuisine comes courtesy of an à la carte and a tasting menu entitled “We are all Meat and Bone”, which also comes in a vegetarian format. Dishes that you won’t want to miss include the pork tartare with its slightly creamy texture and served with crunchy root vegetables of the day, the Ossobuco accompanied by an aromatic onion soup, and the typical Porco San Zé, slow roasted for 6-9 hours, which explains its sublime texture.	habiliragip	\N	\N
3332059229	Cepa	Praça Dos Omaguás	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Terrace		Nessun premio	Farm to table, Home Cooking	3.0	$$	f	t	Cepa is one of those restaurants that always features in food-related conversations in the city, and now it has a new home in the Pinheiros neighborhood! Here, chef Lucas Dante presents creative, seasonal cuisine that prioritises the freshness of ingredients and champions small-scale producers. By his side is sommelier and front-of-house manager Gabrielli Fleming, who is in charge of sourcing biodynamic and organic wines for the cellar that are always the perfect pairing for your choice of dish. We can highly recommend the simply incredible starter of Wagyu tongue with chicken broth, okra and mint.	huberflorin	\N	\N
3332955963	Chef Rouge	Rua Bela Cintra 2238	Brazil	São Paulo	Air conditioning,Valet parking	https://www.chefrouge.com.br/	Nessun premio	French	4.0	$$	f	f	An authentic French bistro, both in terms of its black façade and red awning and its elegant, welcoming interior. The main dining space is complemented by a charming terrace at the entrance, adorned with two impressive trees and with candlelight in the evening. Some of the tables are also occupied by adorable, large red teddy bears that add to the delightful atmosphere. Understandably, the culinary focus and star of the show is classic French cooking with a contemporary touch that is showcased by top-quality ingredients and delicately prepared dishes. These include the grilled foie gras with figs, served with bitter chocolate, and the classic roast duck breast with orange sauce accompanied by gaufrettes potatoes. The incredible Eclair XXL provides the perfect finale!	marianiadrian	\N	\N
3333063435	Café Zi	5F	China Mainland	Beijing	Air conditioning,Car park,Great view,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://www.mandarinoriental.com/en/beijing/wangfujing/dine/cafe-zi	Nessun premio	Cantonese	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	This stylish cafe tells a narrative that is less to do with pushing boundaries and more focused on celebrating traditions. Its concise menu stars all of Canton’s culinary hits – seafood, stir fries, soups… you name it! Each dish rustled up by the veteran kitchen team showcases their shrewd judgment. Also try the refreshing cocktails, interestingly infused with Chinese tea. The views of the Forbidden City from the roof-terrace are also a plus!	basileryan	\N	\N
