3330569582	Akasaka Watanabe	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.akasaka-watanabe.net/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Dressed in white and sporting a bow tie, Yujiro Watanabe greets his guests with a smile. His spirit of graceful service is ‘delicious, fun and welcoming’. Sesame ground in a large bowl, karasumi (salted and dried grey mullet roe) shaved before customers’ eyes and the staging of the kitchen are three shared moments. Rice from Niigata, produced by his sister and her husband, is used. Sea bream chazuke (green tea poured over rice) is a legacy from his mentor. The gratitude is palpable, the conversation lively.	serrakevin	\N	\N
3336169134	Akebonobashi Kazu	16 Arakicho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://akebonobashikazu.com/	Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥	f	t	In search of cuisine that only his native country could conceive, the chef devoted himself to the path of Japanese cuisine. The menu observes the basics of kaiseki while imparting creative flourishes. Wanmono include items with fishcake that complements the broth. Fish in season is salted and grilled over charcoal. Takikomi-gohan, served at the end of the meal, is attuned to the seasons as well. He prizes simplicity yet spares neither ingenuity nor attention to detail.	serafinidmitri	\N	\N
3332562197	Aoyama Ototo	B1F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://aoyama-ototo.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥	f	f	Char-grilled fish and rice cooked in an iron pot are the mainstays here. Set lunches of salt-grilled mackerel and sablefish saikyo-yaki (fish marinated in white miso and grilled) are popular. In the evening, guests can choose from a selection of meats and vegetables to grill. Pickled dried Nanko-ume apricots from the chef’s native Wakayama are the perfect companion to rice. À la carte offerings are plentiful, so pairing various fish and bar snacks with sake and wine is a fun way to dine.	melnikovstella	\N	\N
3334241426	Arakicho Tatsuya	10 Arakicho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The owner-chef prides himself on being the link between diner and food producer. He visits not only the producers of the ingredients he uses, but also the potters who make his crockery, sake breweries and wineries, building strong and trusting relationships. To convey that background to his guests, the chef goes the extra mile to make the dining experience fun. Meals conclude with risotto-like rice soup made with broth from takiawase. The chef is determined to waste nothing, using every morsel of food—a commitment he delivers on with flavours that warm the soul.	grafpaola	\N	\N
3335626160	Asakusa Nagami	4-43-6 Asakusa	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://asakusanagami.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Asakusa is the stage on which chef Takumi Nagami performs. Staying faithful to the native flavours of his ingredients, he brings to bear the seasonal elegance he learned in Kyoto. Soup stock is lightly seasoned so as not to overpower the main ingredients. Two options are available for appetiser platters: drinking snacks adorned with leaves or served in hollowed-out yuzu fruits. The kaminari-okoshi sweet rice puff snack is used as batter for fried items, in a tip of the hat to the chef’s beloved Asakusa.	baranovrachel	\N	\N
3332476605	Azabu Wakei	2-7-9 Nishiazabu	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://wakei.tokyo/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥¥	f	f	The aim here is to serve Japanese cuisine that stands the test of time. To add an inventive touch to fare handed down over generations, repeated innovations seek new taste sensations while preserving the basics. Decoratively arranged sashimi consists of conger eel grilled over straw and dressed with caviar^ tuna is coupled with a starchy nori seaweed sauce. The owner-chef’s principle is that “cuisine is made by people to be eaten by people”. Wakei seijaku, or ‘harmony, respect, purity and tranquillity’, a spirit of mutual respect between chef and guests, pervades the atmosphere.	gavrilovnora	\N	\N
3337476693	Chiso Koryu	5F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://chisou-koryu.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The restaurant’s name declares that its food is created, not merely prepared, and indeed the cuisine here is infused with imagination. Kazuki Takagi spares no effort to furnish a delightfully creative bill of fare, casting his magic on ingredients from around the country. Puffer fish spring rolls, monaka wafer cake filled with monkfish liver and smoked daikon pickles testify to his inventive spirit. Sesame tofu and sea bream on rice with Japanese tea poured over it follow tradition.	voicucatu	\N	\N
3332784128	Edomae Shibahama	2-22-23 Shiba	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	t	A stylish eatery serving the popular fare of Edo. To discover the original dishes of the city, Hiroshi Kaibara dived deep into the literature. ‘Mukimi-kiriboshi’ (dried daikon strips in clam soup stock), a staple of the restaurant, was widely enjoyed during the late Edo period as a favourite to pair with rice. Shiba shrimp, so named because it was originally caught in Shiba inlet, is served in soup as a fishcake. Soup stock brewed from dried bonito flakes, without using kombu, is another traditional taste of Edo cuisine.	salajakub	\N	\N
3337999481	Ginza Adachi Naoto	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	This restaurant values traditional techniques honed at a ryotei. The painstakingly prepared appetisers are a must-try. Seasonal iimushi, mochi rice topped with fish and steamed, is served as an amuse-bouche, paired with seasonal tastes such as sea urchin, salmon roe and chestnut. Instead of rice, the meal closes with konnyaku soba (devil’s-tongue noodles), to relieve uncomfortable fullness after the meal. Meticulous food preparation eloquently conveys the spirit and feeling of the owner-chef.	frolovjulia	\N	\N
3339406812	Ginza Asami	8-16-6 Ginza	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.ginza-asami.co.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Kenji Asami is particular about the way he seasons his foodstuffs. Sesame tofu is blended with vegetables in season. Dashi stock derives its umami   from Rishiri kombu kelp^ soup dishes and takiawase are light-flavoured. At both lunch and dinner, the meal ends with the restaurant’s famous seabream chazuke. The seabream is cut thick so patrons can appreciate its springy texture. Rich sesame dipping sauce is mixed with sesame paste, walnuts and cashew nuts to bring forth the aroma.	polakcristina	\N	\N
3336538137	Hibinoryori Viola	4-12-4 Minamiazabu	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning	https://viola-hiroo.com/	Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	A mother-daughter team opened this restaurant out of a desire to spread Japanese culinary culture. The chef and author of the menu is the mother, herself the daughter of the head of a tea ceremony school. The guiding principle is to base the fare on daily Japanese home cooking. Soup stock is drawn with painstaking care from select seasonal ingredients. The salad of cooked vegetables in season is generously portioned, while steaming fresh-cooked rice is served with Japanese comfort food. Home-style fare infused with the spirit of kaiseki.	ferraririccardo	\N	\N
3334589638	Ichiu	2-22 Kagurazaka	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	t	A door of cedar bark, roof tiles for a nameplate, and a rope curtain with the same diagonals as the ceiling. ‘Ichiu’ means ‘under one roof’, and the ceiling is shaped like a roof to emphasise this point. The menu is faithful to the spirit of Chef Koichi Hamano, a man experienced in Japanese cuisine, particularly sushi. The evening begins with a cup of sake^ soup and fried dishes are then served. After the sushi, takikomi gohan - rice with a variety of ingredients concludes the meal.	veselyolivia	\N	\N
3336605880	Kabukizaura Masashi	4-11-9 Ginza	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	t	Diners are greeted by the open owner-chef and the graceful proprietress. The playful riot of colours on the wall is a tableau on the theme of people’s relationship with food. In this vision stretching from the past to the future, faces are drawn in kumadori, the shaded kabuki makeup fitting for a restaurant ‘behind the kabuki theatre’ (kabukiza-ura). The omakase set meal abounds in changes in inflection: two pieces of nigiri and two types of fried items (tempura and deep-fried) highlight contrasting textures. The chef’s experience with Chinese cooking bursts from his baked gyoza. For the best drink pairings, let the proprietress choose.	nagyemma	\N	\N
3338158832	Kagurazaka Marutomi	3-4 Fukuromachi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	f	f	Wagyu beef is the tie that binds the family of Kenichi Onodera. He named his restaurant ‘Marutomi’ after his grandfather’s cattle farm, and his parents ran a butcher’s shop in Ichinoseki. For that reason, he focuses on wagyu from Iwate Prefecture. Char-grilled and fried items are on the menu, as is sukiyaki with edible wild plants and matsutake mushrooms. The menu changes twice monthly, so a wide range of ingredients are sourced. The combination of traditional multi-course meals with beef dishes is pure Onodera.	bernardiliam	\N	\N
3336866028	Kaiseki Ohara	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The husband and wife in chef’s whites welcome you to their Japanese restaurant. They learned their skills at a chakaiseki (tea ceremony cuisine) establishment. While he works his culinary magic, the proprietress makes confectioneries and serves guests. Decoratively arranged sashimi of white-fleshed fish is served with a mix of rich and light soy sauce^ hassun appetisers are laden with rich varieties of seafood. The spirit of the house shines in dishes that are uncluttered, meticulously crafted and honest.	schneidermihai	\N	\N
3338154836	Kappo Ryu	B1F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://kappou-ryu.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Aiming to conjure dishes that leave an impression, the chef creates through a process of addition, umami upon umami, flavour upon flavour. Tsukuri is combined with grated onion and Tosa vinegar jelly. Boiled-down sake used in grilled items is mixed with dried sardine dashi to bring out its grain flavour. The menu eschews soups and appetiser platters in favour of wagyu beef dishes. Beef tongue in dashi vinegar and cutlets wrapped in bread are the chef’s pride and joy.	grimaldimorko	\N	\N
3331153833	Kappo Yuichi	1-6-10 Kamiogi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	This counter-style restaurant hangs out its shingle in a corner of the roundabout in front of the north entrance to Ogikubo Station. Chef Kotaro Tachibana bases the menu around vegetables, showcasing the depth of Japanese cuisine. Takiawase highlight the ever-changing faces of nature, with colourful vegetables that cool you in summer and warm you in winter. Appetiser platters arrayed with salad with mashed tofu dressing, seasoned boiled greens and vegetables dressed with sesame sauce calm the soul still further with their savoury flavours.	amicowilliam	\N	\N
3331155681	Kiyosumi Takahara	2-15-4 Kiyosumi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://kiyosumi-takahara.instatry.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Kyoto cuisine has left its mark on chef Shimon Takahara. Like his father before him, he apprenticed as a chef in Kyoto, devoting himself to his gastronomical studies. Guided by the teachings of his mentor, Takahara dedicates his heart and soul to creating dishes that offer a sense of the seasons and reverberate in the senses. Each day’s appetiser, prepared from what is most seasonal, shows this devotion. In spring, expect dishes laden with the colours and aromas of daily variations such as bamboo shoots and asparagus.	salajakub	\N	\N
3337307480	Kyoryori Aun	1-33-6 Tomigaya	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.tablecheck.com/ja/shops/aun/reserve	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Seasonal menus are supported by dashi broth drawn from ripened Rishiri kombu. Chef Eiji Onoyama uses dashi in everything, from appetizers to the final takikomi-gohan, rice seasoned with various ingredients. sashimi dishes are prepared in a jellied dashi broth style. They may be complemented with ume or nori seaweed, depending on the type of fish. Egg-yolk soy sauce is the secret ingredient that brings out the umami in tuna sushi. While the soup and takiawase add inflection, the focus is on the power of dashi broth to accentuate the flavour of the ingredients.	benettisergey	\N	\N
3332797273	Miyake Akira	3-8-12 Roppongi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://miyakeakira.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥¥	f	t	The Gifu Prefecture–born owner-chef uses his restaurant to showcase the crafts and foodstuffs of his native region. Born to a family that runs a pottery studio in Tajimi, he serves his dishes on Mino-ware vessels he made himself. Eels are beloved by the locals as they are thought to provide the stamina to overcome the famously sweltering heat of Tajimi. A bowl of fragrant grilled unagi on a bed of rice makes a fine end to the meal. A menu replete with delicacies, such as small bowls of caviar and other seafood on rice, crab served in small pots and char-grilled wagyu beef, lifts the spirits.	alfieriigor	\N	\N
3334652174	Miyawaki	1-3-28 Mita	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Interesting wine list		Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	This counter-style Japanese restaurant dips deeply into Kyoto culinary traditions. Kenta Miyawaki’s experience manifests in fish dishes grilled in soy sauce, sake and citrus slices, or with Saikyoni, served on Kyoyaki vessels. Deep-fried tofu mixed with thinly sliced vegetables is standard fare, preserving the work of previous generations. Ingredients, which include mollusc, unagi, mushrooms and lily bulbs, change with the season. First-time customers receive omakase set meals only^ from their next visit they can order from an extensive menu.	siliprandipiergiorgiofranco	\N	\N
3331302327	Noto Kanazawanosachi Ginza Furuta	5-10-11 Ginza	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://noto-jidaiya.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥	f	t	The couple in charge of this restaurant want to tell the world about the wealth of food from Ishikawa Prefecture. Fish arriving from Nanao, the proprietress’ place of birth, is prepared by her husband, the owner-chef. In addition to vinegared mozuku seaweed and rosy sea perch cooked whole, the restaurant serves mountain treats such as vegetables from Kaga and edible wild plants from the Noto Peninsula. The couple tour farms and sake breweries, building warm relations with producers.	caldarolacagilio	\N	\N
3339739768	Oryori Horiuchi	9-15 Arakicho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The starting point for Mrs. Sayaka Horiuchi is her early life lived amid natural beauty. Considering that ‘the mountains water the village and feed the ocean,’ the dishes are prepared with a spirit of gratitude for ingredients from land and sea. Horiuchi nurtures relations with food producers nationwide who share her vision. Local vegetables are from farmers in Nerima, clams from fishermen in Kyoto. Her famous chicken offal stew is home cooking from her native Yamanashi Prefecture.	medvedevkatie	\N	\N
3337659647	Oryori Ichiho	3F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.ichiho-tokyo.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The name is a play on ‘ho’, which could mean ‘step’, as in ‘stepping forward’ or ‘bounty, prosperity’, reflecting chef Ryusuke Yamazaki’s desire that his restaurant blossom and prosper. The soup stock gets its umami from kombu, with a light flavour reflecting his Kyoto experience. Dishes such as fish steamed with grated turnip on top and dumplings made from bamboo-shoot derive from ages past, recounting the culinary culture of the ancient capital. Pike conger in summer and eddoes in winter are eagerly awaited.	vitalimercuzio	\N	\N
3331817534	Oryori Katsushi	3F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The owner-chef’s obsession with dashi soup stock was learnt from his mentor, a Kansai native. He mixes dashi of dried bonito flakes, kombu kelp and clams to impart depth and richness. This dashi is found throughout: in savoury egg custard, soup dishes, hot pots and other Japanese favourites. The chef’s choice of career is down to the influence of his brother, a sushi chef, and this brotherly bond is shown in his vinegared rice, simmered abalone liver sauce and pressed chub mackerel sushi. The menu is a stage on a fascinating journey that we’re going to keep watching.	pirijiri	\N	\N
