3334406021	Raisan	5-9-9 Yoyogi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The name derives from the masterwork of French epicure Jean Anthelme Brillat-Savarin, The Physiology of Taste, translated as Bimi Raisan. Raisan’s owner was deeply impressed by how timeless gastronomy is. His guiding purpose is to serve dishes light on salt that diners won’t weary of eating. Each dish uses a particular type of dashi stock, including shrimp, dried fish and shellfish. Raisan means ‘praise’, and we praise the warm spirit pervades the service here.	galassiniccolo	\N	\N
3336967278	ROPPONGI RIAN	4F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The concept of this kappo is ‘as much as you want of what you want’. Seasonal fruits and vegetables are lined up on the counter, ready to be cooked by your preferred method. The head chef honed his craft in Kyoto, so he uses rice and water from Kyotango. Pike conger and oysters are sourced from Nagasaki, reflecting his Amakusa roots. The champon served to close out the meal is a recipe from his mother, who runs a shop specialising in this noodle dish.	bianchiandrea	\N	\N
3333322637	Ryuen	7F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.ryuen-tokyo.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The name means ‘swallows in the willow trees’ and it encapsulates the hope that guests will come back to Ryuen just as the swallows return to the willows each spring. The chef’s apprenticeship taught him reverence for ingredients^ he learned the skill of using everything in food preparation, wasting nothing. Beef dishes are served as a middle course, taking after Oniku Karyu, Ryuen’s sister restaurant. Each dish is inventive while preserving the traditions of Japanese cuisine. The chef’s quest for new flavours embodies the spirit of onko-chishin: discovering the new by studying the past.	marchettimarko	\N	\N
3333698120	Sakuragi	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	What Izuru Sakuragi prizes is the deliciousness born of freshness. Soup stock is made with dried bonito flakes shaved in front of the guests. Some items are toasted over straw, others grilled over charcoal, at the chef’s discretion. Freshly cooked rice is served steaming, with accompaniments chosen for contrast in flavour. The thoughtful timeliness with which Sakuragi asks if guests would like seconds is a welcome touch. The chef’s character and passion shine in his cuisine.	molnarbianca	\N	\N
3338685922	Shirokane Shin	3-2-3 Shirokane	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	t	t	The chef queues up the first few items with a narrative arc in mind. Guests then order whatever they like from the menu, creating a programme of their own. That is to say, the chef decides the start of the meal, then guests freely request portions and flavours according to their whims on that day. With omakase seemingly everywhere these days, this show of mutual respect testifies to the thoughtfulness of the chef.	coppolaartem	\N	\N
3332200659	Shizuru	3F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥	t	t	A kappo with service by a couple in chef’s whites. Having both studied Kyoto cuisine, this husband-and-wife divide cooking duties between them. The husband prefers subtler seasoning, always to be accompanied by local sake from Yamagata. The rice, Koshihikari from Uonuma, is delivered from the proprietress’ hometown, cooked in a broad-rimmed cooking pot and patiently transferred to a wooden container. The aroma of incense wreathes the shop in a gentle, quietly soothing Japanese ambience.	caprinivaleria	\N	\N
3337741442	Shokudo Uyuki	4-11-9 Asakusa	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The characters for dining hall can be read as ‘jikido’ in Buddhist terminology – a hall where monks take their meals. Yuki Kanazawa chose the name because he aspires to offer respite that soothes people’s souls through cuisine. He eschews luxurious ingredients and sprinkles his menu with simple fare such as o-hitashi (a boiled vegetable side dish) and aemono (a salad of cooked vegetables). Running his restaurant alone, the chef is never far from his customers. Good-humoured service stimulates lively conversation.	munteanuclaudia	\N	\N
3332904015	Tempura Taku	5F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	3.0	¥¥¥	t	t	Tempura is required to be light. The flour is aerated as it is blended to spread the batter thinly and evenly^ safflower oil is used for the same reason. Service alternates seafood with vegetable items, so flavours are constantly changing. In addition to favourites such as shrimp and conger eel, the menu includes a few novel tempura items. Sea urchin is served rare, so is wrapped in nori seaweed. Asparagus tips and stalks are fried at different temperatures for the best texture.	puglieseadam	\N	\N
3330466870	Sudachi	B1F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://sudachi.tokyo/	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	¥¥¥	t	t	This Japanese restaurant offers puzzles and coded menus! The puzzles rely on a coded-language tradition called goroawase, so if you know a little Japanese, they are sure to raise anticipation. Counter seating, which affords a view of the food-preparation process, adds a touch of fun as well. The eatery’s name can mean ‘leaving the nest’, and indeed the young cooks create a cheerful atmosphere. Puzzle-solving is for evenings only^ lunch is traditional kaiseki, finishing with sea bream rice with green tea poured over it.	antonovrebecca	\N	\N
3331671230	Takahashi	1-7-6 Morishita	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.rest-takahashi.com/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	After studying kaiseki cuisine, the chef moved to Singapore to spread awareness of Japanese cuisine. He returned out of renewed appreciation for the quality of Japanese ingredients. Exceptionally particular about his ingredients, the chef refuses to trust them unless he inspects them at market himself. He learned the basics at famous restaurants in Japan, then acquired hospitality skills from his experience overseas. Both qualities reveal themselves in his fare and service.	fabbromikhail	\N	\N
3332130509	Tsunokamizaka Koshiba	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The owner-chef learned his trade from a master of the Kansai school, and that experience shines in his dashi stock enriched with kombu kelp and his amuse-bouche of rod-shaped pressed sushi of chub mackerel. Appetisers are indispensable to the menu out of respect for time-honoured craftsmanship, but also to delight guests with a cornucopia of drinking snacks. A wide variety of grilled items and rice dishes offers plenty of choice. Diner satisfaction is the top priority here.	ionescuamy	\N	\N
3336399478	Tsurutokame	B1F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://tsurutokame.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	t	t	The name means ‘crane and turtle’ and derives from a Japanese folktale in which a crane from the wider world (the guest) befriends a turtle in a pond (the chefs). It reflects the ladies’ desire to spread Japanese culture through Japanese cuisine, so appetiser platters are arranged like the decorations of traditional events. The gracious service puts you immediately at ease. The crane and turtle are a symbol of good fortune. At birthday parties, happiness is sure to descend on your table.	bellandigiorgio	\N	\N
3333488146	Uisane	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	t	What Kohei Ikeda tries to do is mix freewheeling imagination into traditional fare, an approach taught by the head of Kikunoi during his apprenticeship there. Fine examples include sea urchin wrapped in sea bream and spring rolls of glass shrimp. He focuses on ingredients from his native Ehime, the better to share its wonders with the world. Born and raised in Uwajima, Ikeda dreamed of becoming a chef in his youth. He never forgets to carry out this ‘original intention’, a phrase reflected in the name of the restaurant.	reinhardtgabriela	\N	\N
3337943444	Waki Shun	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.wakishun.com/	Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	What Shun Miyahara wants is to convey is the power and vigour of ingredients in season. His determination comes from his mentor. Pride of place on the menu goes to char-grilled items. Grilling fresh-caught fish and wagyu beef, Miyahara works the flame with constant minute adjustments, aiming for the precise moment when the flavour is fully revealed. His experiences in Paris come out in an interior blending Western design with Japanese influences, and in sparking conversations with his guests.	ozmax	\N	\N
3331756661	Washoku Ebihara	19 Iwatocho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://wasyoku-ebihara.jp/	Nessun premio	Japanese	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The shopfront lettering sums up this couple’s approach: ‘Enjoy the season’. The proof is in the earnest cooking of the owner-chef and the cheerful disposition of the welcoming proprietress, his wife. Taking a page from his experience as a caterer of chakaiseki (tea ceremony cuisine with its roots in Kyoto), seasonings are both delicious and nutritious. Garnishes and pickled vegetables are inventive, teaching us that true flavour is found in the subtler notes.	carusoleonardo	\N	\N
3336705119	HASUO	5-10-3 Hiro	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning	https://www.instagram.com/newkoreanhasuo/	Nessun premio	Korean	4.0	¥¥	f	f	The fare here is New Korean: traditional Korean cooking with a modern interpretation. The chef applies the techniques and sensibilities he learned studying Western cooking. Banchan with 16 side dishes is a revelation. The Imperial Crepe adhering to the five flavours and five colours, is a brightly coloured treat. Ganjang-gejang, sauce-marinated blue crab, is marinated lightly to suit modern tastes. Hasuo proclaims a new Korean cuisine, raising the food of the chef’s Korean homeland to a new level.	cellericelina	\N	\N
3333883090	ETHICA	1-3-3 Ginza	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.ethicatokyo.jp/	Nessun premio	Mexican	4.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The chef studied in Mexico to learn about a culture that had been evolving for millennia. Based on the natural and spiritual features he sensed there, he created recipes to bring to mind local ingredients and culinary methods. The ‘three jewels’ of Mexican cuisine are maize, beans and chilli peppers. Combining this trio with Japanese ingredients is Ethica’s speciality. Out of concern for the environment and society, the chef aims to minimize food loss at his restaurant.	caputostefan	\N	\N
3334581672	OLD NEPAL	1-42-11 Gotokuji	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning		Nessun premio	Nepali	5.0	¥¥¥	t	t	The restaurant’s name is a shout-out to the culinary culture of Nepal in the good old days. Street scenes of the capital Kathmandu adorn the walls. Keen to convey the mood of old Nepal, the chef uses his own experiences to spin tales on the theme of journeys. Of particular note in his set menu is the creative flair of appetisers. They’re followed by Dal bhat, a dish enjoyed by mixing the flavours of lentil soup, rice and curries – a Nepalese journey of the palate.	pesciroberto	\N	\N
3330185905	The Pizza Bar on 38th	38F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Car park,Counter dining	https://www.mandarinoriental.co.jp/	Nessun premio	Pizza	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	As the name suggests, food is served at a counter, which seats just eight. Prix fixe is made possible by starting each seating at the same time. A succession of different pizzas are baked, then cut into eight, allowing diners to enjoy a slice of each type. The dough is kneaded from organic Italian wheat and rested for hours, creating a moist crust with a light texture. This is a popular place, so make your reservations early.	balazsaurora	\N	\N
3337313972	Soan Mitate	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Mitate means ‘the three freshes’: freshly ground, freshly kneaded and freshly boiled. Like the chef, most of the ingredients come from Toyama, and his experience with Japanese cuisine is evident. The set menu includes buckwheat-flour galettes of tuna and caviar as well as an assortment of delicacies served before the soba, which is are slender, cleanly cut 100% buckwheat noodles. Watching his practiced movements as he prepares the soba is an added entertainment. An impressive list of champagnes is also offered.	rinaldisamuel	\N	\N
3338768090	Tamawarai	5-23-3 Jingumae	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Credit cards not accepted		Nessun premio	Soba	5.0	¥¥¥	t	t	The chef is of the view that soba’s deliciousness is determined by its flour. He grinds husked buckwheat both from his garden and from farmers in Ibaraki Prefecture, catching every iota of the fresh-ground aroma. Coarse ground seiro (soba served on bamboo wicker) is full of wild flavour. Tofu soba, inspired by staff meals, and hot seiro with hot tsuyu sauce and beaten egg, are unique taste experiences. Evening service is prix fixe only, with treats such as buckwheat mash and rolled omelettes included.	rinaldisamuel	\N	\N
3337709785	Yuan Yamori	3-9-7 Tsukishima	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Credit cards not accepted,Shoes must be removed		Nessun premio	Soba	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Omakase set meals showcase the appeal of soba in all its glory. An assortment of stone mortars used to make different types of soba flour attest to the chef’s attention to detail. Buckwheat dumplings are kneaded from freshly ground flour. To keep an eye on the flour and ensure that no heat is added, buckwheat is slowly and carefully ground. Soba topped with nori seaweed is rich with the aroma of broth and nori, while mori soba is placed in the hands rather than on the table so you don’t miss any of the pure fragrance of buckwheat. Through the aroma and act of slurping rediscover the essence of soba noodles.	testagabriel	\N	\N
3334485195	eman	1-3-6 Shirakawa	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://eman.jp/	Nessun premio	Spanish	4.0	¥¥¥	t	t	Satoru Kobayashi expresses his connections with the seasons and food producers of Japan through the medium of Spanish hometown cooking. A speciality is rice cuisine. Clams on rice, a Basque dish, is done in a Fukagawa-meshi style (asari clam miso soup over rice). For the squid-ink paella, firefly squid are used. Familiar Japanese rice is harmonized with the styles of each region. The sheer variety of the menu, encompassing tapas, char-grilled dishes and more, testifies to the depth and richness of Spanish cuisine.	munteanuclaudia	\N	\N
3331088921	MASIA	8F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Car park,Counter dining	https://masia.jp/	Nessun premio	Spanish	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	Masía is Spanish for ‘farmhouse’. The chef, Mateu Villaret, created the restaurant from his roots, the dining table of his family back in Catalonia. Inspired by the Mediterranean Sea and the Pyrenees, the hometown fare combines ingredients from both sea and mountain – and proves that Japanese seafood and Spanish ham and chorizo go very nicely together.	rivaalexei	\N	\N
3337108748	Tinc gana	4-3 Rokubancho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://jeromequilbeuf.jp/	Nessun premio	Spanish, Contemporary	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	To convey the gastronomy of contemporary Spain, the chef adopted the concept of Barcelona's local cuisine with a modern sensibility. Pan con tomate is just one Catalan tradition. All ingredients are painstakingly selected to whet the appetite with their lightness. The restaurant’s name means ‘I’m hungry’ in Catalan.	ferraririccardo	\N	\N
3334777538	Ishibashi	3-6-8 Sotokanda	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Cash only,Shoes must be removed		Nessun premio	Sukiyaki	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	Ishibashi began as a butcher shop in the early Meiji period. The owners also kept a sukiyaki shop next door, and this restaurant has passed down its original flavours through the generations. The fifth-generation owner-chef chooses wagyu beef by its marbling and the quality of the red meat. Seating is tatami-mat only^ cooking is done exclusively by one hostess. In a unique touch, the meal ends with ojiya (flavoured rice gruel) made with fluffy eggs. The old-fashioned atmosphere adds to the flavour.	sorrentinosantiago	\N	\N
3338176835	SUKIYAKI ASAI	3-11-15 Toranomon	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sukiyaki	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	Experience sukiyaki hospitality at a counter. Standing right across from you, the server expertly prepares alternating slices of beef and vegetables. Sourced from a distinguished butcher in Shiga Prefecture, the choice beef is served in a variety of thicknesses. On the side are the signature dipping sauces: meringue-like beaten egg and rich egg yolk mixed with Tosa vinegar. The meal culminates with a final dish of shirataki noodles, stir-fried in warishita sauce and beef fat. Each dish will enchant both your eyes and taste buds.	benesemily	\N	\N
3339744693	Hikarimono	7-5-11 Roppongi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥	t	t	Kan Sato, known affectionately as ‘Satokan-san’, loves hikarimono (literally ‘shining thing’: fish with silver skin, sliced with the skin left on), hence the shop’s name. The menu is composed with drinkers in mind, so this hikarimono is never left off. Sato’s offering of three courses—sushi and bar snacks, mainly nigiri, and bar snacks—as well as à la carte is pleasing. Hikarimaki of pilchard, shredded dried plum and bettarazuke (daikon pickled in salted rice malt) is famous. Sato’s aim is to offer a sushi shop where people drop in casually. The value for money truly shines.	dragomirpippo	\N	\N
3331695432	Sushi Ichigo	4-18-7 Kasuya	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.sushi-ichigo.jp/	Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	‘Ichigo’ means ‘ranking in the top class’, proclaiming the chef’s ambition to stand among the top ranks of chefs. The menu proceeds from drinking snacks to grilled items and steamed dishes, then finally to sushi. Sushi rice is cooked in a broad-brimmed pot at high heat to permeate the rice with the red vinegar^ this emphasises the rice grains while drawing out the flavour of the toppings. As he forms his sushi by hand, he engages earnestly with the guests lining the counter.	salluis	\N	\N
3335169788	Sushi Ikki	2-29-21 Sakuragaoka	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The menu is a judicious balance of sushi and other Japanese dishes. The meal begins with meticulously refined sesame tofu and bowls of basic soup stock. To vary the tone, the sushi is interspersed with savoury egg custard and flame-broiled offerings. For the first piece, Chef Takafumi Kiyonaga likes to serve young Pacific sea bream, demonstrating the marination, knife techniques and other aspects that make up his craft. Red-fleshed fish is paired with mustard and conger eel with pepper, testifying to the influence of other types of Japanese cuisine.	bellandigiorgio	\N	\N
3333777204	Sushi Kagura	3-2-3 Shimbashi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://kagura-shimbashi.com/	Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The chef was drawn to sushi by the simplicity of a dish that is complete with only vinegared rice, wasabi, and a topping. Labouring as an apprentice from dawn to dusk, Kagura’s chef gained a thorough grounding in work that was faithful to the basics. As he moulds his sushi, the chef adds a unique interpretation to the techniques he learned.	zaitsevvictoria	\N	\N
3338789303	De Pefferkär	49 route d'Esch	Luxembourg	Huncherange	Air conditioning,Car park,Interesting wine list,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus,Wheelchair access	https://de-pefferkaer.lu/home.html	Nessun premio	Modern French, Classic Cuisine	5.0	€€€	t	t	An elegant restaurant adorned with vibrant modern artwork. The boundless energy of the lady of the house and her son can be felt in their cuisine. Building on a classical repertory, they don’t hesitate to introduce modern textures and techniques and the owner-wine waiter will suggest fine local wines.	popescumaya	\N	\N
3334271696	Sushi Kanesho	3-33-9 Asakusa	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://search.app/k4azLtu6hMao4NKY9	Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥	t	t	Akira Watanabe inherited old-school sushi traditions but learned from his mentor the spirit of adding his own novel spin. Vinegared sushi rice is regarded as the most important component of great sushi, and Watanabe’s evolved over time through trial and error. The chef displays judgment and skill as he serves gizzard shad first, and his simmered conger eel and kurakake egg (egg straddling the rice like a saddle) reflect his personality. He confides that he tries to ‘investigate the old and understand the new’.	locatelliflorian	\N	\N
3332840347	Sushi Kobayashi	8-2-10 Ginza	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	t	t	Guests are greeted in Edo dialect with a hearty “Irasshaimashi”. A short shop curtain hangs under a market-stall roof, lending the shop an Edo-era food-stall ambience. Expect steaming hot towels, piping hot tea and gracious service. Influenced by two mentors, the owner-chef prepares fish patiently in the time-honoured way and uses red vinegar to form rice portions. His aim is to be his patrons’ every-day sushi stop. An old-school, no-nonsense sushi shop in both appearance and demeanour.	lombardiemanuele	\N	\N
3332532836	Sushi Marufuku	3-17-4 Nishiogiminami	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://sushimarufuku.com/	Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Aged sushi prepared with originality and passion. Yutaka Isayama ages fish to bring out aroma, tenderness and flavour, favouring dry-ageing to impart a certain wildness of taste. Snacks are interspersed so as not to jade the palate with a succession of intensely flavoured sushi toppings. Aged sushi pairs well with sake, so ask the proprietress, an accomplished sake sommelier, for suggestions.	santoronicola	\N	\N
3338721398	Sushi Matsumoto	1-49-14 Itabashi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Notable sake list		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The atmosphere is relaxed, with jazz playing in the background. To ensure the most enjoyable sake experience, the chef alternates between snacks and nigiri. This is a great place to try a variety of sakes, served in glasses and accompanied by an extensive selection of snacks and sushi. Toro (fatty tuna) is served first, so that the heat of the freshly cooked vinegared rice melts the fat.  Fresh-toasted nori and tea served in Nambu ironware teapots show commitment without compromise.	ferrettivladislav	\N	\N
3336018619	Sushi Miura	6-19-46 Akasaka	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The wall hanging, calligraphy by the chef’s mentor in Kyoto cuisine, says ‘forthrightness’, recalling the Buddhist exhortation to remember a sincere and honest heart at all times. Kenta Miura opened his sushi restaurant to bring his studies of Japanese cuisine to life in every sushi piece. A master of the basics, he interposes them with seasonal delicacies such as young pepper leaves with bamboo shoot and sweetfish smoked in pine needles. The career of a talented chef unfolds before you.	moldovanmelly	\N	\N
3331364069	Sushi Miyuki	1-15-11 Shintomi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The owner-chef of Sushi Hashimoto opened this restaurant for young people to showcase the spirit and skills they learned from him. Sushi is cut thick and portioned generously. At lunchtime, only nigiri is served, but both nigiri and side dishes are offered in the evening. Hiroyuki Hashimoto keeps a watchful eye over his young charges, to see that they grow to become chefs in their own right. The name is an amalgam of one Chinese character each from Hashimoto’s parents’ names. Graceful service by artisans devoted to their craft.	vogelbeatrice	\N	\N
3335805184	Sushi Rinda	2-24-12 Shimomeguro	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥¥	t	f	The unconventional restaurant name is in honour of the owner-chef’s daughter^ probe further, and you find that Rinda is also the name of a song by the chef’s favourite rock band. With such rock-star inspirations, it’s no wonder that the young cooks are such a blur of motion. The fatty flesh of tuna is sliced thin and piled high before the sushi is moulded so the fat will melt faster. ‘Rindamaki’ is a sumptuous roll sushi of tuna, sea urchin and salmon roe. The lively enthusiasm of the restaurant’s team is sure to lift your spirits.	carlinikita	\N	\N
3333188497	Sushi Satoru	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Retired pro boxer Satoru Araki is testing his mettle in a new arena. The backbone he developed from keeping his weight under the cut-off helped him overcome the struggles he faced moving from boxing ring to sushi counter. He excels in firing up the crowd. Straight from the bell, he offers eggplant sushi with a jaunty ‘oide nasu!’, a pun on the words ‘welcome’ and ‘eggplant’. Focusing on tuna, he forms the vinegared rice reverently in his fist. You’ll be knocked out by his charm and passion.	cernyfrida	\N	\N
3334163228	Sushi Taichi	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	This restaurant loves the chic style of Edo culture and values the human touch. Many sushi shops these days offer two time-limited seatings, serving only omakase set meals. However, Sushi Taichi shows the mettle of an old-fashioned sushi chef. The owner-chef’s agreeable disposition ensures him many regular customers. Above the counter is a shop curtain, while the walls feature woodblock prints depicting street stalls. If you like old-school sushi shops, Sushi Taichi is for you.	grimaldimorko	\N	\N
3337804799	Robin's Teppanyaki	2F	Taiwan	Taipei	Air conditioning,Car park,Interesting wine list,Valet parking	https://www.regenttaiwan.com/dining/robins-grill	Nessun premio	Teppanyaki	4.0	$$$$	f	t	One of the best known teppanyaki places in town, named after a long-serving maître d’, enjoys an enthusiastic local following. The show-stopping teppan tricks are something to behold, but they don’t outshine the perfectly cooked seafood, premium steaks from the U. S. , or the Wagyu from Japan and Australia. The six-course dinner unfolds according to your pace as experienced chefs time things just right. An excellent wine list also features.	friedrichteresa	\N	\N
3335563366	Sushi Teru	7 Arakicho	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	A brightly plastered wall catches your eye in this alley in Shinjuku Ward’s Arakicho before the cheerful owner-chef ushers you in. Service is omakase set meals only. Snacks are simple, honest fare such as seafood sashimi and grilled items. Sushi is presented in a definite order, starting with lighter flavours and working gradually toward richer ones: first white-fleshed fish such as gizzard shad, then tuna followed by conger eel, for example. The shop is named after the owner-chef’s grandmother^ she stayed hale and hearty, so the name is a wish for the restaurant to have a long life.	salajakub	\N	\N
3334917921	Yi Shun (Macau)	381 Avenida de Almeida Ribeiro	Macau	Macau	Cash only		Nessun premio	Street Food	4.0	$	f	t	A longstanding shop offering traditional desserts, such as pineapple with crushed ice and sweet lotus seed soup with egg. Milk custard and ginger milk pudding are house specialties, to which you can add lotus seeds or red beans for extra flavour.	stracciaantonio	\N	\N
3339211828	Sushi Yuki	5-17-4 Hiro	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥¥	f	f	This sushi restaurant moved to Hiroo from its original location in Yokohama’s central Kannai district. When Yuki Hayashinouchi became the shop’s third-generation owner-chef, he named it after himself. He continues the distinctive long, narrow, streamlined sushi pieces that he learned from a sushi shop in Ginza. Devotees of Hayashinouchi’s mentor can see from the chef’s comportment that the two have much in common. He infuses his individuality and character into every sushi piece he forms. The model temperament of a sushi chef.	haascamilla	\N	\N
3331479605	Sushidokoro Yamato	3-7-2 Tsukiji	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	Growing up immersed in the rhythm of his father's sushi restaurant, the chef nurtured a childhood dream of becoming a sushi chef. After apprenticing in Tokyo, in gratitude he gave his shop the same name as his father’s restaurant. Omakase set menus lean towards nigiri, fulfilling the duty of a true sushi restaurant. Rice is seasoned with a mixture of red vinegar and rice vinegar to accommodate a wide range of sushi toppings. The first item is gizzard shad, an ingredient that taxes the skill of any sushi artist. Generous portions of rice lend presence to each item.	rivaalexei	\N	\N
3336233094	Sushiya Hajime	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://sushiyahajime.wordpress.com/	Nessun premio	Sushi	5.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The character for ‘sushi’ in Sushiya Hajime refers specifically to fermented sushi, harking back to the beginnings of sushi culture. ‘Hajime’ means ‘beginning’, expressing the chef’s desire to see the sushi world with a newcomer’s eyes, as when he used to be sent to select fish at the market. Fish is prepared according to ancient practices such as salting and simmering. Omakase set meals consist of numerous pieces of sushi formed small, affording rich variety and commitment to quality is uncompromising, which is deeply satisfying. Finely honed skills shine in attentive service.	veselyolivia	\N	\N
3333330030	Takumi Sushi Owana	4F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥¥	t	t	Kenji Oana and his fellow artisans work with practiced synchrony, alternating servings of nigiri and drinking snacks. Rice is seasoned with either red or white vinegar^ sushi of tuna and pickled daikon radish is shaped like o-hagi (rice cakes coated with sweet red bean paste), a legacy inherited from his mentor along with the shop’s name. Snacks are delightfully inventive: baked and steeped barracuda and eggplant, for example, or conger eel grilled with Saikyo miso. Never knowing what to expect accentuates the fun.	egorovzoe	\N	\N
3334716623	Tomidokoro	6-13-3 Shimbashi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Cash only - lunch,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	A place to feed on sushi to your heart’s content. The owner-chef, a man of few words, focuses strictly on sushi to maintain the proper tempo. True to his Obihiro roots, he cooks ancient varieties of Hokkaido rice in a broad-brimmed hagama pot. Fish is cut thick, so guests can enjoy the flavour to the fullest, and vinegared rice servings are generous to match. Standard lunchtime items are a bargain. The chef is generous both in sushi and in spirit.	morozovmegan	\N	\N
3334253012	UDATSU SUSHI	2-48-10 Kamimeguro	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus	https://www.udatsu-sushi.jp/	Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	A fresh intersection between sushi and art. The walls of this modern space are hung with paintings by artists, giving the feeling of dining in a gallery. Bright and colourful side dishes include herb rolls, smoked tuna and veggie rolls. Nigiri technique hews close to the basics, but with some tempura-inspired creative touches, such as sea urchin and fried nori. A sushi restaurant for a new age.	bernasconikarl	\N	\N
3335143535	TEMPURA & WINE SHINO	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Diners enter from the ground-floor café. Ascending the staircase beside the piano takes you to a space like the darkness lit by the full moon. ‘Tempura with wine’ is the concept here, with the focus on à la carte dining, with food presentation accompanied by descriptions of the day’s tempura. Among the natural wines are those based on agricultural methods that track the movements of celestial bodies. The atmosphere is mystical, as if tilting a glass of wine in the company of the moon.	demurtasarianna	\N	\N
3335462228	Tempura Asanuma	6F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.tempura-asanuma.com/	Nessun premio	Tempura	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Fascination with Edo culinary culture led this chef to open a restaurant in Nihombashi. Knowing that Edokko (natives of Tokyo) would eat thick-battered tempura soaked in sauce, the chef uses the technique completely opposite to the thin coating he learned as an apprentice. But the thicker coating traps air bubbles, imparting a fragrant nose and light texture. In an homage to the port of call where tempura arrived from Portugal, the fried feast begins with shrimp toast, a speciality of Nagasaki.	tarasovangela	\N	\N
3335449569	Tempura Nakagawa	2-14-2 Tsukiji	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The chef learned his tempura from a master versed in the old ways. The first tiger prawn is fried at just the right heat to bring out its aroma^ the second is served rare to elicit sweetness. Conger eel is fragrantly fried with minimum sesame oil. Nakagawa’s trademark theory is that ‘tempura is a process of drying-out’, by which he means ingredients are steamed and grilled in their batter to remove moisture, bringing their flavour to the fore.	marinotommaso	\N	\N
3337755612	Tempura Otsuka	2-12-11 Iriya	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	3.0	¥¥	t	f	The owner-chef, born and raised in Iriya, is an artisan through and through. Proceedings are by the book, starting with milder flavours like tiger prawn, squid and sillago before proceeding to bolder tastes. Edomae toppings are favoured, such as tidepool gunnel and big-eyed flathead. Decorating the walls are senshagaku, wooden tablets typically posted on the pillars of shrines, inscribed with local supporters’ names. The characters suffuse the restaurant with the atmosphere of old neighbourhoods.	vogelbeatrice	\N	\N
3333400152	Tempura Shimomura	1-11-13 Misuji	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	5.0	¥¥¥	t	f	The menu is extensive to cater to diners’ tastes but has strong regional roots. Fish and vegetables are fried in alternation, with the accent on shrimp, sillago and conger eel. Recently, few tempura places offer tendon only, but here, tendon of vegetable tempura and kakiage (all-in-one seafood and vegetable tempura blend) are available at lunchtime. Feel free to add seafood items to the vegetable set menu. Woodblock prints and an icebox vaguely recreate the atmosphere of working-class Edo.	romaniriccardo	\N	\N
3337579015	Ginza Yondaime TAKAHASHIYA	4F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://takahashiya-ginza.com/	Nessun premio	Unagi / Freshwater Eel	3.0	¥¥	t	t	This restaurant was founded in Sugito, Saitama Prefecture, in 1873, at the dawn of the Meiji period. Charmed by the allure of Tokyo, Akihiro Takahashi, the fourth proprietor, fulfilled a dream by hanging out his shingle in Ginza. As his main store’s concept was to be an ‘eel kappo’ he serves soup dishes and decoratively arranged sashimi in the kaiseki style. Eel is freshly cut, steamed and grilled to achieve a tender texture, in the Kanto style. Dipping in egg yolks, à la sukiyaki, is one of his creative ideas.	mazziandrei	\N	\N
3330529653	Ishibashi	2-4-29 Suido	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Notable sake list,Shoes must be removed	https://unagi-ishibashi.com/	Nessun premio	Unagi / Freshwater Eel	4.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Near the edge of the Kanda River stands a traditional detached house. The red-brick wall harks back to a time when such things were regarded as symbols of Westernisation. Unaju (broiled eel on a bed of rice in a lacquerware box) includes a whole eel^ the Deluxe version includes one and a half eels. With only a small difference in price, we recommend the latter. The eel is cut and slowly steamed only after you order, so expect to wait an hour for your dish to arrive. Spend the time absorbing the restaurant’s history, learning about eels or enjoying a snack or two with drinks.	vargalucia	\N	\N
3330275547	Ăn Ði	3-42-12 Jingumae	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Interesting wine list	http://andivietnamese.com/	Nessun premio	Vietnamese	3.0	¥¥¥	t	f	Modern Vietnamese food from a chef trained in French cuisine. Ăn Ði combines seasonal Japanese ingredients with bánh xèo, raw spring rolls and phở to express the four seasons of Japan with Vietnamese cuisine. With a skilled sommelier at the helm, you can also expect perfect pairings that elevate each dish. The emphasis is on natural wines, but the restaurant also showcases a modern approach to pairing sake and shochu. The restaurant name means '’Bon appétit!’ in Vietnamese.	costantinitomas	\N	\N
3330142950	124. KAGURAZAKA	3F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Yakitori	3.0	¥¥	t	f	The numbers in the name are both the address of the shop and the birthday of a member of staff. The first skewer is chicken heart, because the deep, rich flavour of this cut makes a strong impression. norimaki (bite-size chunks wrapped in nori seaweed) of chicken tenderloin, cooked vegetable salads and deep-fried tofu intersperse the omakase set meal, varying the tone and providing items that pair agreeably with sake. Ramen in chicken broth closes out the presentation. Served in dragon-pattern bowls with nori to evoke a certain sense of nostalgia.	tohnjohn	\N	\N
3333369368	Asagaya BIRD LAND	3-37-9 Asagayaminami	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.asagaya-bird-land.com/	Nessun premio	Yakitori	5.0	¥¥	t	f	Piped jazz fills the air at this little counter wrapped around a grill. When his mentor let him open up his own branch, the chef put down roots in Asagaya. Emulating his mentor, he grills Okukuji Shamo chicken, pairing it with wines. Prix fixe menus alternate between chicken and vegetables. The value of the rare chicken cut known as the ‘oyster’ was revealed in this area as well, leading to its popularity. Free-range chicken as you’ve never tasted it.	schmittelisa	\N	\N
3333402110	BIRD LAND	B1F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Interesting wine list	https://ginza-birdland.sakura.ne.jp/	Nessun premio	Yakitori	3.0	¥¥	f	f	BIRD LAND enchants guests with its wonderfully distinctive yakitori. With supple imagination and a wealth of experience, Toshihiro Wada crafts a new world of possibilities for the humble skewer.  Included among the skewers in his set menus are liver paté, chicken grilled in pepper and oyakodon (chicken and egg on rice). Always attentive to customers’ voices, he reveals the value of rare cuts such as chicken oyster. Determined to pass on his skills, he often works the grill with apprentices at his side.	pesciroberto	\N	\N
3336065349	Yakitori Hinata	2F	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Yakitori	4.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The owner-chef is passionate about the locally raised chicken of the Tamba-Sasayama region. The concept of yakitori with wine suggests some intriguing pairings^ particularly compatible with wine are seared chicken breast dusted with salt from Guérande and liver brushed with balsamic sauce. The appetiser is chicken-liver pâté. The restaurant’s name hearkens back to the names of areas and relatives with whom the couple shares a bond^ it connotes a ray of sunshine on a Kurayamizaka, which translates to ‘darkness hill.’	predapriamo	\N	\N
3333096663	Yakitori Honda	3-5-1 Minato	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Yakitori	4.0	¥¥¥	f	f	The boundless curiosity of Chef Takuya Honda shines in his yakitori. The Hinai chicken he uses is aged to bring out the umami. Honda constantly tinkers with his fare through trial and error to elicit the best flavour from each cut. Unimpressed by the usual, narrow choice of salt or sauce, he broadens the selection to include condiments like condensed soy sauce, balsamic soy sauce, and rice vinegar with herbs. The chef similarly puts effort into recommending the best wine pairings.	haascamilla	\N	\N
3334916605	YAKITORI Moe es	7-13-10 Roppongi	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Yakitori	4.0	¥¥¥	f	t	The chef’s pedigree in both yakitori and French cuisine shines in this restaurant’s omakase set meal. The magic begins with appetisers such as smoked chicken or terrine de foie gras. The chicken soup is like a clear consommé. The restaurant sources whole chickens of a variety that requires a long breeding period, and rests the meat for several days to concentrate the flavour. To bring out the flavour of the meat, salt is used liberally, sauce sparingly. Simple chicken soba noodles end the meal.	montanarijack	\N	\N
3330811038	Jimbocho Gokita	2-4-9 Nishikanda	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://jinbocho-gokita.com/	Nessun premio	Yakitori, French	3.0	¥¥¥	f	t	Yuto Gokita is pioneering yakitori eaten with French sauces. In place of yakitori dipping sauce, Gokita favours Madeira and red-wine sauces prepared from chicken bones and calf broth. Skewers of chicken breast and shrimp are smothered in sauces of crustaceans. Skewers of chicken meatballs are accented with herb butter for a Bourgogne flavour. Reservations are required for the set menu which includes wood-grilled lamb and pigeon.	palombasauro	\N	\N
3338433165	YŌSHOKU BISTRO TŌYAMA	1-11-13 Nishiazabu	Japan	Tokyo	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://ueno-tooyama.jp/nishiazabu-tooyama/	Nessun premio	Yoshoku	5.0	¥¥	t	f	The symbol of YŌSHOKU BISTRO TŌYAMA is a mural of Chef Tadayoshi Toyama at work in the kitchen. The interior is enfolded in warm wood panelling, the blackboard menu a touch of bistro charm. Yoshoku in ‘Yoshoku bistro’ means Western-influenced cooking, and the name references Toyama’s longstanding love affair with Western dishes and his experience studying French cuisine. The accent here is on dishes that pair well with wine. From yoshoku to French, the chef’s time and effort are rewarded with the depth of his menu.	riccidaniele	\N	\N
3330983381	Kest	Valmieras iela 1	Latvia	Cēsis	Car park,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus,Wheelchair access	https://www.kest.lv	Nessun premio	Modern Cuisine	5.0	€€€	t	t	The town’s former fire station is now home to a restaurant which provides an experience of two parts. You start in the bright restaurant, where you dine from a 5 course tasting menu made up of modern dishes which keep local produce to the fore – and this is accompanied by an interesting alcoholic drinks flight which also includes cocktails. After dessert, you head through to the dark, moody lounge for the second part of your night: a coffee and time spent over a relaxing post-meal drink. The team really do run the place with genuine warmth.	marinescumario	\N	\N
3332724901	H.E. Vanadziņš	Rīgas iela 15	Latvia	Cēsis	Car park,Terrace	https://vanadzinarestorans.lv/	Nessun premio	Traditional Cuisine, Regional Cuisine	4.0	€€	t	t	You’ll find plenty of locals here, from the serving team to the regular guests, which creates a welcoming vibe and a convivial feel. The lengthy menus cover all bases, from flavour-packed soups and salads to delicious flatbreads and hearty mains inspired by northern Latvian cuisine. There’s a lovely terrace hidden to the rear that's worth seeking out, and bedrooms are available should you wish to stay the night.	mazzaviktor	\N	\N
3334054393	36.Line	Baznīcas iela 2B	Latvia	Jurmala	Air conditioning,Great view,Interesting wine list,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://36line.com	Nessun premio	Modern Cuisine	3.0	€€	t	f	Beachside locations don’t come much better than this, as you can walk straight in off the sand. Those after some respite from the sun can stay inside, while those wanting to feel the sea breeze should head for the glass-fronted terrace. The accessible menu covers all options, from lighter sushi and tartares to dumplings and hearty grilled fish and meat dishes. The tiramisu laced with the famous Black Balsam – their national drink – is a must-try, and the extensive selection of wines and cocktails is perfect for watching the sun go down.	ionescuamy	\N	\N
3334698984	MO	Friča Brīvzemnieka Iela 7	Latvia	Liepaja	Air conditioning,Terrace		Nessun premio	Traditional Cuisine	3.0	€€	t	t	Beside the promenade in the historical canal area sits Julianna’s Courtyard, where the 19C printing house and later 20C factories have been lovingly restored to create one of Liepāja's go-to areas. Here you’ll find MO, which offers a satisfying meal out at a fair price. Ask for a table either on or overlooking the terrace, then choose from the wide-ranging menu that includes tartares, carpaccios, sharing plates and main dishes such as beef with truffle mash. Locally produced beers and cocktails make the perfect accompaniment.	urbanalessia	\N	\N
3337384395	Pavāru māja	Pilsoņu iela 2	Latvia	Līgatne	Car park,Garden or park,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus	https://www.pavarumaja.lv	Nessun premio	Modern Cuisine, Regional Cuisine	3.0	€€€	f	t	A former maternity hospital on the edge of a rural village plays host to this smart, modern restaurant. Here, you are encouraged to relax in their lovely nature observation garden – a place to sit back, listen and watch – before taking time to really savour your meal. Their raised beds and foraging trips provide the kitchen with vegetables and herbs, while the rest of their produce is locally sourced. The 4 course lunch and 8 course dinner are informed by what’s fresh and available each day.	rusnakhannah	\N	\N
3332003230	Stage 22	Aspazijas bulvāris 22	Latvia	Riga	Air conditioning,Great view,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://www.kempinski.com/en/grand-hotel-kempinski-riga/restaurants-bars/stage22	Nessun premio	Japanese	4.0	€€€	f	f	Set on the top floor of the Grand Hotel Kempinski Riga, this sumptuous restaurant affords superb views out over the park and the city skyline. Start with a drink in the chic cocktail bar or out on the lovely narrow terrace, then settle into plush chairs to enjoy your evening. This is an ideal place for celebrating and you will be looked after with consummate ease by the professional team. The wide-ranging Asian menu includes the likes of spicy tuna roll and Argentinian beef fillet with shallot cream, while the crispy vegetable tempura with dashi sauce is not to be missed.	giordanopietro	\N	\N
3332019268	COD	Tērbatas iela 45	Latvia	Riga	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.cod.lv	Nessun premio	Japanese Contemporary	4.0	€€	t	t	Make for the sultry basement bar for a cocktail or two before heading back up to the atmospheric ground floor restaurant with its dim lighting and on-trend soundtrack. The chefs here might be Latvian, but the cuisine is contemporary Japanese. Start with a selection of sushi and tempura, then choose something from the robata grill. The gyoza ‘of the day’ is a must, the black cod with sweet miso is a hit, and the mochi ice cream is the perfect way to finish. There’s a vibrancy and freshness to the cooking, yet it still respects the fundamentals of Japanese cuisine.	rusnakhannah	\N	\N
