3331413632	Nicos	Cuitláhuac	Mexico	Azcapotzalco		https://www.nicosmexico.mx/	Nessun premio	Traditional Cuisine, Mexican	4.0	$	f	t	Nicos was once a humble stand selling sodas and snacks until one day a customer saw employees eating something delicious and the restaurant was born. The son of the founder now serves as chef, overseeing a tasting menu of classic Mexican cuisine features such standouts as taco de cecina crujiente on a corn tortilla that delivers characteristic intensity. and a flavor-packed, bright dish of camarones al tamarindo. An enchilada stuffed with tasado and beans and bathed in a pixtle sauce from Oaxaca is an aromatic, tasty crowd-pleasing dish, while the aguachile comes with a feisty kick thanks to fiery chili peppers.	zaitsevvictoria	\N	\N
3331113723	Atarraya	Guerrero 633 Brisas de Zicatela	Mexico	Brisas de Zicatela	Terrace	https://restaurante.covermanager.com/atarraya/	Nessun premio	Mexican	5.0	$$	f	f	Is there a cooler restaurant in Puerto Escondido? Channeling the sleekness of a Japanese zen garden, the outdoor space is picture-perfect and built out with handsome wood beams, a backlit bar, and an old tree that stretches across the dining room. The cooking is familiar fare, seasoned and presented with the right amount of care: Crisp tostadas stacked with pan-fried ribeye, squares of tuna rolled in sesame seeds, and vibrant salsas that upgrade everything they touch. You could make a meal out of the appetizers alone or press on with a chile relleno stuffed with vegetables and blanketed in a smooth manzano pepper sauce. Though the cocktail offerings are succinct and stick largely to the classics, the bartenders shake and stir with notable skill.	bernasconikarl	\N	\N
3336790821	Arbol	Transpeninsular Km. 19.5	Mexico	Cabo San Lucas	Interesting wine list,Terrace	https://www.rosewoodhotels.com/en/las-ventanas-los-cabos/dining/arbol	Nessun premio	Indian, Asian	3.0	$$$$	f	t	Located within Rosewood's Las Ventanas al Paraiso, this beguiling setting hosts diners in an indoor/outdoor space arranged with chunky blond wood tables on a polished stone patio. Lantern-hung branches cast a rosy glow.The large menu is impressively prepared and is founded on Indian specialties. However, the range of offerings is further enhanced by charcoal-grilled meats and seafood, as well as an abundance of Asian specialties. The Punjabi fish masala features the catch of the day, which is gently cooked in a zesty, moderately spiced tomato-based sauce enriched with ground cashews and flecked with herbs and spices. It comes served with spice-tinted basmati pilaf and Indian-style pickled vegetables and is best paired with butter-brushed naan hot off the tandoor.	virgolinifrancesco	\N	\N
3331890983	Los Tres Gallos	Leona Vicaria Esquina 20 de Noviembre	Mexico	Cabo San Lucas	Terrace	https://lostresgallos.com/	Nessun premio	Mexican	3.0	$$	f	t	This popular operation commands a corner of downtown Cabo San Lucas. The sprawling space is centered around a grand courtyard, and the setting, with its brick structure, paver stone patio, and wood furnishings, is brightened by pink and red painted stucco. A roof terrace is open during the evening.Los Tres Gallos serves a crowd-pleasing menu of generously portioned traditional Mexican cuisine like chile rellenos, enchiladas, and mole con pollo. The pozole is a vivid broth enriched with roasted chiles and tomatoes. Stocked with plump kernels of toothsome hominy alongside chunks of braised pork, it is served with a host of embellishments. The flan de casa is as big as a house, so plan on sharing dessert.	nerielijah	\N	\N
3333091389	Comal	Carretera Transpeninsular Km. 15	Mexico	Cabo San Lucas	Air conditioning,Terrace,Valet parking	https://aubergeresorts.com/chilenobay/dine/comal/	Nessun premio	Mexican, Contemporary	4.0	$$$$	t	t	Walk through the lobby of the posh Chileno Bay Resort, down a small hill and along a dramatic walkway with swings and lounge furniture to arrive at this open-air restaurant with beautiful views of the water. The contemporary Mexican menu is inventive with a clear personality that spans the country's distinct regions while also pulling in influences from South America. Octopus kastakan is a ceviche-style dish made with thin coins of octopus tossed with crispy cubes of pork belly and crispy pork skin. Served with warm plantain tortillas with slightly sweet notes, it's a brightly flavored dish. Their variation on a Pavlova with strawberry-basil sorbet employs a variety of pastry techniques and is noteworthy.	habiliragip	\N	\N
3331986020	Manta	Carretera Federal 1 Km. 5	Mexico	Cabo San Lucas	Air conditioning,Great view,Interesting wine list,Valet parking	https://www.mantarestaurant.com/	Nessun premio	Mexican, Fusion	5.0	$$$$	f	t	Set within the Cape Hotel in a stunning space that frames picture-perfect views of Cabo's famed Lands End, this modern dining room under Enrique Olvera's umbrella isn't just impressing with its good looks. The contemporary Mexican cuisine is punctuated by clear Peruvian and Japanese influences. Offered as a tasting menu and à la carte, expect bold dishes from a kitchen that's unafraid of bringing the heat. Begin with sashimi or a Peruvian ceviche before savoring a refreshing melon salad with diced honeydew and cantaloupe in a leche de tigre fueled by yuzu. Next, a sweet potato with a tamarind and soy crust in an almond mole is at once simple and elegant. From Korean fried chicken and tempura fish tacos to cochinita served in bao buns, Asian accents run from beginning to end on the à la carte menu.	martinifederico	\N	\N
3338289681	La Casa De Las Mayoras	Calle Guadalupe Victoria y Calle Miguel Hidalgo Donceles 28	Mexico	Cancún		https://la-casa-de-las-mayoras.negocio.site/	Nessun premio	Mexican, Vegetarian	5.0	$$	f	t	Finding a dedicated vegetarian restaurant in a city that aims to please all manner of tastes can be expected, but it’s still a pleasant surprise to find one with this much homey charm and hospitality, not to mention such flavorful, satisfying cooking, regardless of your dietary predilections. Breakfast is the order of business here, with a menu featuring an assortment of chilaquiles, quesadillas and tostadas, boasting hand-made tortillas and astutely seasoned salsas. Tame the spice with “superfood” batidos blended with the likes of organic honey, tropical fruits and chia seeds. To add to the ambience, lucky visitors may have the pleasure of encountering Guato, a local celebrity in the guise of a chihuahua-beagle mix, who sometimes holds court here.	demurtasarianna	\N	\N
3338143358	Kiosco Verde	Av. López Portillo SM. 84 Lote 14	Mexico	Cancún	Car park	https://restaurant-kiosco-verde.blogspot.com/	Nessun premio	Seafood, Regional Cuisine	5.0	$$	t	t	Octavio Santiago continues a longstanding family tradition at Kiosco Verde. This simple but welcoming kiosk was founded by his father in 1974 and is located along the road that crosses Puerto Juarez. The detailed menu is complemented by specials written on the blackboard, but all focus on seafood from the local market and highlight the regional style. The dishes are as tasty as they are colorful, as in the Argentine-style empanadas stuffed with lobster meat and chimichurri sided by baked potatoes, fried leek julienne and aguacate, along with chile hummus. Pulpo zarandeado is another winning dish, and with generous portions you'll be hard-pressed to order dessert—but don't skip it. The specials, like the dreamy panna cotta, are worth it.	tohnjohn	\N	\N
3336283588	Los Danzantes Mexico City	Parque Centenario 12	Mexico	Coyoacán	Air conditioning,Terrace	https://www.losdanzantescoyoacan.com/	Nessun premio	Mexican, Contemporary	3.0	$$	t	t	Located in downtown Coyoacan, Los Danzantes is often busy with a mix of locals and foreigners. Set in a historic building, this restaurant shines a spotlight on contemporary Mexican cuisine, and the menu is packed with a wide variety of dishes. Chef Alejandro Piñom knows what works--the hoja santa with a base of goat cheese and a miltomate sauce with chile, has been on the menu for more than two decades. Filete de res en chichilo has a hint of smoke from the grill but it's the mole that stands out. Empanada de membrillo pairs quince and cheese for a pleasant taste. Their sister spot in Oaxaca shares the same name but offers a different style and menu.	habiliragip	\N	\N
3330588779	Bajel	Paseo De La Reforma	Mexico	Cuauhtémoc	Air conditioning,Car park,Great view,Wheelchair access	http://www.sofitel-mexico-city.com/restaurants/bajel	Nessun premio	Contemporary	4.0	$$$	f	t	Nestled on the ground floor of the Sofitel, Bajel boasts a prime location with stunning views. It's a French brand so they may whisper bonsoir, but one look at the menu and it's clear they're firmly planted here. Inside this sophisticated setting, the fully open kitchen demands your attention. Both a la carte and the tasting menu highlight contemporary Mexican-leaning cuisine with many nods to smoked meats and Spanish influences. Dishes like enchilada de pato confitado, tortilla de epazote and abodo de guajillo are as traditional (and enjoyable) as they come. Barbacoa is tender and plentiful, while dessert is a Basque-style tart with peach ice cream.	mancinialberto	\N	\N
3336807687	Botánico	Alfonso Reyes 217	Mexico	Cuauhtémoc	Garden or park,Terrace		Nessun premio	Contemporary	3.0	$$	t	t	Upon first glance, it may not look like much, but the exterior belies the oasis found within. Tall cacti, a lake stocked with fish and a convivial atmosphere make this a natural choice for date night, but this laidback spot is equally well suited to families and groups too. Everyone enjoys being here as they connect over plates like homemade ravioli with blue crab and a pumpkin cream base, or a simple grilled fish with al dente vegetables. Crème brûlée delivers a strange-sounding surprise with the addition of Kalamata olive, but it's a convincing finale.	puglieseadam	\N	\N
3339024464	Martínez	Puebla 90	Mexico	Cuauhtémoc	Counter dining,Terrace	http://www.martinez.rest	Nessun premio	Contemporary	4.0	$$	f	f	Chef/owner Lucho Martinez was inspired to open this eponymous restaurant by his daughter, Emilia, who once shared that her favorite thing to do in Paris was eat in the bistros with him. It sparked an idea—why not recreate that feeling right here in Mexico City—and Martinez is just that: a small restaurant with good food and wine.The ambience is young and bustling. Order at the bar and you'll get a sneak peek into the kitchen. The concise menu features a list of tasty dishes with nods to Mexican, French and even Japanese cuisine. Pollo a la parrilla is a main course hit, while chocolate cake with vanilla ice cream is spot on.	cattaneomiguel	\N	\N
