3336589149	Fiola Miami	1500 San Ignacio Ave.	USA	Coral Gables	Air conditioning,Interesting wine list,Terrace,Valet parking	https://www.fiolamiami.com/	Nessun premio	Italian, Seafood	3.0	$$$$	f	f	Everything seems to sparkle inside Chef Fabio Trabocchi’s Italian looker in Coral Gables. White tablecloths, dark hardwood floors, glass at every turn and a gleaming open kitchen set the stage in a restaurant that is busy any day and time of the week. Seafood, naturally, is the calling card here, and raw bar items like shrimp cocktail with tomato compote highlight quality sourcing. Caviar and truffles can find their way to any dish, if you ask, and housemade pastas like spaghetti alla vongole are portioned to share. From there, the grill does most of the heavy lifting for any whole fish and high-end steaks offered. Even though this is a prime destination for business meetings, the setting is far from stuffy, in part thanks to a suave service team.	brandtclara	\N	\N
3337499383	Serẽa Coastal Cuisine	1500 Orange Ave.	USA	Coronado	Air conditioning,Car park,Garden or park,Great view,Terrace,Valet parking,Wheelchair access	https://sereasandiego.com/	Nessun premio	Seafood, Mediterranean Cuisine	3.0	$$$$	f	f	If the historic “Hotel Del” is the jewel of Coronado Island, then snagging a seat on Serẽa’s sprawling patio is like nestling into said jewel box. Thanks to its stunning views of the hotel's landscaped grounds and the ocean beyond, this is one of the prettiest perches in all of California. Just be sure to snag a reservation, because you’ll have competition from plenty of wedding parties and conference-goers.Deep-pocketed pescatarians will be delighted by the freshness and sustainability of the fish on offer—from inviting platters of raw oysters and cracked Maine lobster to flash-fried red snapper from Baja, offered by the pound for sharing. And even after the sun finally fades on the million-dollar view, the outdoor fireplace sets an exquisite mood.	melnikovstella	\N	\N
3338470797	Burmatown	18 Tamalpais Dr.	USA	Corte Madera		https://www.burmatown.com/	Nessun premio	Burmese	5.0	$$	t	t	Bypass the tired Asian-fusion offerings and head straight for the authentic Burmese dishes at this out-of-the-way cutie. A nutty, crunchy and flavorful tea leaf salad is the perfect answer to a hot summer day, while hearty spiced potato-stuffed samosas and fresh, springy egg noodles tossed with barbecue pork and fried garlic chips will warm your soul in the cooler months.Given the high quality of its food, it’s no surprise that Burmatown is Corte Madera’s most popular neighbor: it’s big with local families from the surrounding residences who pack every single table, attended to by warm servers. If you’re willing to make a special trip to this charming bright-orange bungalow, the laid-back vibe will have you feeling right at home.	romanosimone	\N	\N
3334107443	Vaca	695 Town Center Dr.	USA	Costa Mesa	Air conditioning,Car park,Counter dining,Terrace,Valet parking,Wheelchair access	https://www.vacarestaurant.com/	Nessun premio	Spanish, Contemporary	5.0	$$$	t	f	Courtesy of Chef Amar Santana, this cool and rustic tapas bar offers consistently pleasing food and an extensive list of sherries and Spanish wines amid a quirky setting (the banquettes are decorated with jokes). In addition to the beautiful bar area, guests may sit in two outdoor areas or the main dining room with views into the semi-open kitchen. The lunchtime prix fixe is a great deal, though it would mean skipping their wonderful array of tapas and paellas. Standouts include the bikini sandwich, layering cured Jamón Ibérico, Manchego cheese, and a bit of truffle oil into pretty little piping-hot grilled sandwiches. Croquettas de pollo tucked with shredded white meat are perfectly seasoned and fried to a golden crisp.	grimaldimorko	\N	\N
3330320814	Destroyer	3578 Hayden Ave.	USA	Culver City	Air conditioning,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus,Terrace	https://www.destroyer.la/	Nessun premio	Contemporary	5.0	$$	f	f	The architecturally intriguing Hayden Tract district is the perfect backdrop for Jordan Kahn's adventurous Destroyer, located just across the street from sister restaurant, Vespertine. The minimalist interior keeps your attention on the food, where each plate is a complex composition of flavors that are bound to delight and surprise. Begin with such harmonious starters as avocado confit, a half avocado dusted with their onion ash and served with confit tomatoes and dressed arugula that is simple but impressive. Koshikari rice porridge delivers depth and a riot of textures, while the milk chocolate crémeux finished with almond streusel, praline and a dusting of frozen cucumber cream is a striking closer.	rinaldisamuel	\N	\N
3334223596	Juliet	8888 Washington Blvd.	USA	Culver City	Air conditioning,Interesting wine list,Terrace	https://www.juliet.la/home	Nessun premio	French, Contemporary	5.0	$$$$	f	f	Chic and French go hand in hand, especially at Juliet in Culver City. This gorgeous space has a contemporary French aesthetic with as much seating outdoors on the posh patio as indoors. The open kitchen buzzes all day, but dinner is where this team shines. Oysters, tartare or tuna carpaccio could kick off the meal, but the mousse au foie de volaille, with its delicate tart filled with chicken liver mousse, toasted hazelnuts and an apple gelee, is an auspicious beginning. Sea bream with ratatouille and pistou is simple but well executed, and pairs well with one of their many wines available by the glass. Don't skip dessert—whether you choose the éclair or the crème fraiche cheesecake with passion fruit gelee, it's a perfect ending to a delightful meal.	costasergio	\N	\N
3339487126	Stock & Barrel	316 W. Davis St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Interesting wine list,Terrace,Valet parking,Wheelchair access	https://stockandbarreldallas.com/home/	Nessun premio	American	5.0	$$$	t	f	Set in the charming Bishop Arts area, Jon Stevens's Stock & Barrel is the perfect neighborhood spot. With an industrial chic design, open kitchen, and convivial bar counter, this restaurant is consistently packed with locals, all here for the inviting ambience. Whether you sit at the bar or in a booth, the friendly staff will ensure an enjoyable time. Expect American and Texas favorites that have been given a twist, as in deviled eggs with pickled rhubarb, meatloaf made with wagyu beef, or a bread pudding from croissants. Flavors are big and bold. Don't miss their caramelized Brussels sprouts charred with chili and cilantro. The wine list features a nice selection of by-the-glass offerings.	montanarijack	\N	\N
3333426724	Written By The Seasons	380 Melba St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Terrace	https://www.writtenbytheseasons.com/	Nessun premio	American Contemporary	3.0	$$$	t	t	You don’t need to be a genius to work out the philosophy behind this likeable restaurant that is a perfect fit for the Bishop Arts District. Local ingredients in season are used here to create fresh tasting, colorful, and rewarding dishes. Vegetables are often given star billing rather than mere supporting roles and the kitchen isn’t afraid of incorporating a little international flavor here and there. So, carrots may come with dukkah, branzino with harissa, and pork ribs with miso^ and be sure to start off with some of their delicious focaccia. The time of year not only informs their menu but also their cocktail list, so it’s worth exploring. The space is bright and contemporary and the atmosphere, warm and energetic.	hofmannelena	\N	\N
3333714082	Crown Block	300 Reunion Blvd. E.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Credit cards not accepted,Great view,Valet parking	https://crownblockdallas.com/	Nessun premio	American Contemporary, Meats and Seafood	3.0	$$$$	t	f	Set atop Reunion Tower, this large circular restaurant makes the most of its stunning views with floor-to-ceiling windows, and with a bit of luck you’ll be seated in the right area to see the sun set. To complement the sophisticated setting, a dress code means your fellow diners will be looking equally sharp. “Rare Steaks, Fine Seafood” is their tagline and the menu doesn’t disappoint—along with a healthy selection of sushi there’s plenty of produce from local farms. If you do go for one of their first-rate steaks don’t miss the excellent triple-cooked fries. The restaurant opened in its current guise in 2023 and proved an instant hit, so you need to book early, despite it seating over 200.	neagufilippo	\N	\N
3338663189	Fearing's	2121 McKinney Ave.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Brunch,Car park,Terrace,Valet parking	https://fearingsrestaurant.com/	Nessun premio	American, Regional Cuisine	4.0	$$$$	t	f	Nestled inside the luxe Ritz-Carlton, Fearing's is elegant and dramatic. The handsome look may read traditional steakhouse but this flagship restaurant from Chef Dean Fearing isn't straightforward. Instead, the American menu with clear Southwestern influences is both complex and composed with buffalo and antelope along with classic-leaning filets. The dishes are surprising, offering bold flavors and new takes on the tried-and-true. Case in point? Their Lone Star mosaic, Fearing's take on carpaccio with tuna, wagyu beef, salmon, and barbecued eel composing a vivid plate resembling stained glass. Baklava S'more riffs on the campsite classic with puff pastry piped with milk chocolate pot de creme, pistachio marshmallow, and burnt rosemary ice cream for a refined finale.	egorovzoe	\N	\N
3330140152	Quarter Acre	2023 Greenville Ave.	USA	Dallas		https://www.quarteracrerestaurant.com/	Nessun premio	Contemporary	5.0	$$$	f	f	Here the name embodies a pastoral suburban ideal, while the strikingly chic space flaunts a certain airy forest-meets-island aesthetic—both making nods to Chef/owner Toby Archibald’s New Zealand roots. Along similar lines, his cuisine walks a line between flashiness and substance, featuring broadly international flavors anchored by classic French techniques, while showcasing eye-catching presentations and an occasional flair for the dramatic. Take for example an indulgent savory éclair filled with king crab, topped off with a soigné ruffle of feather-light smoked avocado mousse and tangy dots of Meyer lemon, or a seabass grilled “en papillote” inside a banana leaf, served with a vibrant mango salsa and a creamy macadamia nut sauce.	grimaldimorko	\N	\N
3335030064	Rye	1920 Greenville Ave.	USA	Dallas		https://www.rye.restaurant/	Nessun premio	Contemporary	4.0	$$$$	f	f	Located in Dallas's Lower Greenville district, Rye sports a sexy, cozy vibe with wood plank floors, beamed ceiling, and exposed red brick walls. Guests are cared for with a genuine hospitality and the beverage selection is especially impressive with a range of cocktails on offer (they also operate Apothecary, the cocktail lounge next door).Food-wise, the cuisine is unrestrained and offers diners a taste of wide-ranging inspirations. The à la carte menu serves up the likes of a Texas-raised wagyu beef hot dog or a dish of comforting cacio e pepe. The tasting menu is an extensive affair that may combine a beef tallow and mezcal emulsion drizzled taco, a slice of beet and goat cheese lasagna, and chocolate cake dressed with spruce and rosemary.	valentimatteo	\N	\N
3336417313	Mister Charles	3219 Knox St.	USA	Dallas		https://themistercharles.com/	Nessun premio	Contemporary, American	3.0	$$$$	t	t	The sweeping, opulent space sets the tone for an indulgent experience—this is a place where guests dress to impress and should expect an extravagant meal. The menu spans classic as well as contemporary luxury, blending French and Italian fare, from carpaccio to crab cakes to corn and truffle beignets. Diners can nibble on canapés like a lobster roll on a petite brioche bun, or sample fusilli with caviar and fontina, and might proceed to more substantial fare, including showy tableside presentations like Dover sole with lobster beurre blanc, or a Texas wagyu beef Wellington. Adroit service is combined with skillfully made cocktails and a substantial wine list, while the signature baked Alaska provides a dramatic grace note.	szabosofia	\N	\N
3335809945	Knox Bistro	3230 Knox St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Brunch,Terrace,Valet parking	https://knoxbistro.com/	Nessun premio	French	5.0	$$$	t	f	There’s an experienced team of French émigrés behind this bright and breezy bistro and it shows in all departments. This may be a French bistro—and one that’s a perfect fit for sophisticated Knox Street—but it doesn’t overdo the Gallic decoration. Instead, it concentrates on offering authentic and satisfying classics that’ll have you dreaming of weekends in Montmartre in no time. Lunch sees a simpler menu but one that includes a fine rillettes de poisson and a selection of oysters. At dinner all the French bistro favorites appear, like lapin à la moutarde or canard à l'orange^ and for dessert, the kitchen really shows its skills with a textbook Paris-Brest that you won’t want to share with anyone.	cellericelina	\N	\N
3336726811	Mercat Bistro	2501 N. Harwood St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Brunch,Counter dining,Terrace,Valet parking	https://www.mercatbistro.com/	Nessun premio	French, American	4.0	$$$	f	t	There are occasions for trends and then there are times when tradition reigns, and that's exactly where Mercat Bistro comes in. This amiable restaurant delivers the classic French bistro experience from its wood floors, marble-topped tables, and round-backed chairs to its extensive menu showcasing all of the hits. Discover a litany of favorites, ranging from escargots in parsley butter and French onion soup to moules frites and pot au crème. Come at lunch to indulge in sandwiches like the croque monsieur. In true bistro fashion, nothing is too precious, but it's all comforting and delicious. The service is welcoming and attends to the mix of business diners and locals with skill but be sure to secure a seat in the room with the open kitchen to enjoy a livelier vibe.	mazzaviktor	\N	\N
3337490646	Barsotti's	4208 Oak Lawn Ave.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Counter dining	http://barsottis-dallas.com/	Nessun premio	Italian	5.0	$$	t	f	Set at a busy junction in the Oak Lawn neighborhood, this popular restaurant is named after the owner's great-grandfather. The contemporary trattoria's bright and bold design is eye-catching, with black-and-white floors and a curved bar with counter seating for taking in views of the open kitchen.Simple and straightforward is the name of the game here, where the emphasis is on terrific homemade pasta dishes. Linguini with shrimp scampi, lasagna Bolognese, or the ever-comforting spaghetti with meatballs are just a few of their hits. Add a selection from the all-Italian wine list to complete the experience.	silvestritommy	\N	\N
3339313895	Monarch	1401 Elm St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Great view,Interesting wine list,Valet parking	https://www.monarchrestaurants.com/	Nessun premio	Italian-American	4.0	$$$$	t	t	Monarch is the restaurant for that special date or celebration^ for when you want to put on your best gear and make the evening a special one. If you’re a good planner or willing to promise to spend a certain amount, you could even secure one of the window tables that take advantage of its perch on the 49th floor of The National and the far-reaching views. The menu runs the gamut from Italian to American and includes plenty of steaks and premium seafood. Concentrate when ordering though, otherwise you might end up paying a king’s ransom. Wood-fired dishes are the specialty, whether whole branzino, tiger prawns or chicken^ pasta dishes like short-rib Bolognese are also good^ and the wine list is a weighty tome with all the big names.	demurtasarianna	\N	\N
3335717943	Tei-An	1722 Routh St.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Car park,Counter dining,Notable sake list,Valet parking	https://tei-an.com	Nessun premio	Japanese, Soba	4.0	$$$$	t	t	The Arts District and Tei-An are a perfect fit, as this Japanese restaurant provides an elegant and cultured experience in an aesthetically striking environment. Opened back in 2008, it has also played an important role in nurturing the city’s love affair with Japanese cuisine. The menu is varied and extensive, allowing diners to decide their own level of expense and experimentation. There’s everything from tonkatsu to tempura, as well as dishes like grilled freshwater eel (which comes with its crisped spine as you’d get in Japan), but its house-made soba and udon noodles—served cold or hot—are the house specialties. They come with an array of seasonal toppings and dipping sauces, many traditional and some, like Bolognese, more original.	sokolovabigail	\N	\N
3337337918	Sachet	4270 Oak Lawn Ave.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Terrace	https://www.sachetdallas.com/	Nessun premio	Mediterranean Cuisine	3.0	$$	t	t	There’s something about this corner restaurant that makes it look so inviting—and sure enough, the moment you step inside you feel enveloped in the warm embrace of the bright, southern European sun. That’s not just down to the light, open feel of the large room, but also because of the charming service team. This may be the baby sister of Gemma restaurant, but the menu here looks to the wider Mediterranean for its influences, stretching from Morocco to Syria and round to Italy, France, and Spain. Dishes like octopus with freekeh and lamb with gigante beans are as colorful and appealing as they are satisfying and nourishing. If you can’t get a table—and the booths are much demanded—you can try your luck at the large counter bar.	neagufilippo	\N	\N
3330250226	El Carlos Elegante	1400 N. Riverfront Blvd.	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Valet parking	https://elcarloselegante.com/	Nessun premio	Mexican, Contemporary	5.0	$$$$	f	f	Don't be fooled by the nondescript exterior, as this lively and friendly place welcomes guests with an appealing rustic and authentic Mexican ambience. Stop in the lounge at the entrance for a drink before heading to your table, though if you enjoy being in the center of it all while watching the team cook over a wood-fired grill, then there's no better spot than a seat at the horseshoe counter. The menu is tightly edited, featuring a handful of dishes ranging from small plates and masas to meatier entrees. Everything is vibrant, full of flavor, and designed for sharing, so order with abandon to sample it all. Their list of tequila and mezcal is impressive, and the helpful staff is always on hand to offer recommendations.	pirijiri	\N	\N
3336528264	Georgie	4514 Travis St.	USA	Dallas		http://www.georgiedallas.com/	Nessun premio	Steakhouse	3.0	$$$$	t	f	This swanky space, decked out in sunny tones of peach and carrot with a retro-meets-modern vibe, certainly looks the part for an elegant night out, especially given the attentive team of uniformed servers. Chef RJ Yoakum is an alumnus of The French Laundry, an influence apparent in his cooking, which combines a reverence for top-quality ingredients with a basis in classic technique along with a hint of playfulness. Take, for example, crispy, creamy potato “churros,” served with a fluffy, creamy caramelized onion dip (and an optional addition of caviar), or a pork tenderloin paired with whipped popcorn polenta and a BBQ cherry gastrique. The prices may be nothing to sneeze at, but guests are certain to be well taken care of—and well fed.	pierimarco	\N	\N
3338131224	Stillwell's	Hôtel Swexan	USA	Dallas	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Credit cards not accepted,Interesting wine list,Valet parking,Wheelchair access	https://hotelswexan.com/dine/stillwells/	Nessun premio	Steakhouse	3.0	$$$$	t	f	It's nestled inside the Hôtel Swexan, a tongue-in-cheek play on Swiss hospitality meets Texas, but there's no mistaking the roots of this upscale steakhouse: it's named for the legendary lady rancher, Hallie Stillwell. Ride the elevator to the seventh floor to enter this clubby den, then sip a cocktail as you peruse their extensive menu with all of the classics. Kick things off with a shrimp cocktail, caviar, or treat yourself to the bountiful seafood tower, but of course, steaks take center stage. There are many selections but their bespoke in-house HWD beef—a superb Akaushi beef from their own breeding program just north of the Red River—is the one to beat. All the while, elegant white jacketed staff keep things moving smoothly.	smirnovjessica	\N	\N
3336441322	Kan Kiin	201 Southgate Ave.	USA	Daly City		https://www.kankiineatery.com/	Nessun premio	Thai, American	4.0	$$	t	f	An unremarkable Daly City strip mall might not be the most obvious spot to find well-crafted Thai cuisine, but putting appearances aside, this cheery little spot delivers on flavor. During the daytime, you’ll find an assortment of fusion brunch items (for example, a take on chicken and waffles that features hat yai, fragrant Southern Thai-style fried chicken) alongside a variety of more typical Thai restaurant fare. The menu includes the usual suspects of stir-fried noodles and curries, as well as less ubiquitous items like spicy clay pot catfish, and across the board special care and bold seasoning sets the cooking apart. For a case in point, try the hand-made curry puffs, which boast an impeccably delicate, flaky crust.	baranovrachel	\N	\N
3336192922	Chai Pani	406 W. Ponce De Leon Ave.	USA	Decatur	Air conditioning,Terrace,Wheelchair access	https://www.chaipani.com/decatur	Nessun premio	Indian, Regional Cuisine	4.0	$$	f	t	Decatur's Chai Pani is a festive place, where strings of lights and colorful artwork keep things high spirited, and the kitchen looks to locally sourced ingredients to create the seasonally minded South Asian dishes. The versatile menu is detailed with enticing descriptions that may just to convince you to try one more dish, and there are plenty of options for family-style dining. Whether dining a deux or with a group, enjoy the likes of butter chicken, that deliciously rich staple of boneless chicken in a tomato-cream butter sauce, or fried okra, with its ever so easy to eat lightly seasoned shards. Gobi pepper fry, a popular hybrid Chinese and Indian dish, is given a unique slant here with battered cauliflower that's both tart and spicy.	palombasauro	\N	\N
3331426484	MARKET Restaurant + Bar	3702 Via De La Valle	USA	Del Mar	Interesting wine list,Valet parking,Wheelchair access	https://marketdelmar.com/	Nessun premio	International	3.0	$$$	f	t	Chef/owner Carl Schroeder is a La Jolla native with an encyclopedic knowledge of the region’s bounty. He’s been celebrating every locally sourced ingredient for over a decade now in this kitchen, and his repertoire of creative cooking continues to draw patrons for both special occasions as well as date night. The dark and seductive décor manages to be elegant without a hint of pretense, but one glance at the parking lot and you know that this spot is favored by a moneyed lot.Sushi is well-loved here, but it would be a shame to miss the chef’s creative and internationally influenced dishes. For instance, blue cheese soufflé with anise-orange marmalade shows off a European bent, while miso-glazed cod with shrimp dumplings is resoundingly Asian.	marianiadrian	\N	\N
3337641731	Mercantile Dining and Provision	1701 Wynkoop St.	USA	Denver	Air conditioning,Brunch,Wheelchair access	https://www.mercantiledenver.com/	Nessun premio	American	5.0	$$$	t	f	They may have their own entrance, but it's worth the walk through Union Station to arrive at Mercantile Dining & Provisions. Chef/owner Alex Seidel presides over this handsome spot complete with wood floors and tables, tufted leather banquettes and a soothing palette of brown and blue. The elevated take on American comfort food wins over guests who dine on starters, pastas and meat-forward entrees like venison and Tomahawk steak. Portions are large, but expect spot-on flavors and executions. Tuck in to a half confit Miller farms chicken with gorgeous golden-brown crispy skin for a dish that feels at once classic and unexpected. To conclude, a flaky galette-style apple pie with roughly chopped, skin-on apples is bested by vanilla ice cream, a Vin Santo sauce and smoked cinnamon.	vitalimercuzio	\N	\N
3334108878	Fruition	1313 E. 6th Ave.	USA	Denver		https://www.fruitionrestaurant.com/	Nessun premio	American, Contemporary	4.0	$$$$	f	f	Although the space itself isn’t exactly ritzy, top-flight service and indulgent cooking combine to make guests here feel like royalty despite the lack of luxury trimmings. Chef Alex Seidel is one of the Denver dining scene’s leading lights, at the vanguard of ingredient-centered eco-conscious cuisine. Whether it's a crisp golden-brown latke served with sweet onion creme fraiche and smoked trout roe, or impeccably seared scallops with creamy farro, the dishes here impress with their strong technique, pristine products and rich flavors, but an element of playfulness is also apparent, as in a fried maitake slider in the style of Nashville hot chicken. For an unfussy finale, chewy, crispy chocolate chip cookies, served warm and melty, are sure to satisfy.	marchettimarko	\N	\N
