3333022377	Snackboxe Bistro	1960 Day Dr. NW	USA	Duluth	Car park		Nessun premio	Lao, Street Food	5.0	$$	t	f	Chef/owner Thip Athakhanh may have moved to the United States as a toddler, but her roots in western Laos come shining through at Snackboxe Bistro. The broad menu also overlaps with Thai and Vietnamese cuisine for a likeable selection of dishes that come piled so high you'll have leftovers for days (hint: bring a friend or three). Plates to not miss include wings coated in a funky-sweet jeow bong sauce with just a hint of spice, and nam khao, a classic crispy rice salad, that is a riot of flavors and textures. Made with fermented pork sausage, herbs, onions and peanuts with fish sauce, lime, rice vinegar and sugar, it's positively delicious. End on a sweet note with a slice of coconut cake or fried donuts with condensed milk.	salajakub	\N	\N
3337035480	Breakwater Barbecue	30 Ave. Portola	USA	El Granada	Air conditioning,Car park	https://www.breakwaterbbq.com/	Nessun premio	Barbecue, American	4.0	$$	t	t	Just about ten minutes north of Half Moon Bay in the charming seaside town of El Granada, Breakwater Barbecue is an all-American spot tucked at the base of a blue shingled house. Walk in and you may be greeted by the butcher slicing and prepping slabs of meat. This is the kind of place where you'll want to grab a beer immediately upon entering before tucking into the likes of chili made with house-ground brisket, smoked short rib and house-made sausage. Braised in smoked pork stock with roasted poblano, smoked tomato and garlic, it's packed with smoky, spicy flavors and best sided with a slice of corn bread. But wait, there's more, specifically the Texas Trinity. It's an impressive heap of brisket, ribs and links, plus two sides (Merle's potato salad and beans and greens are good choices) and bread. Come hungry.	weberdragos	\N	\N
