3337392936	Nihonshuzammai Utsutsuyo	2F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Notable sake list,Shoes must be removed	https://www.ututuyo.jp/	Nessun premio	Izakaya	5.0	¥¥	t	f	The name of the restaurant is inspired by the expression ‘is this a dream or reality?” and embodies the desire for you to enjoy sake and good food to your heart’s content with a touch of enchantment. The sheer range of local sakes on offer can make choosing hard. Start with an appetiser and decoratively arranged sashimi with your first cup. The drinking snacks are varied too, from tofu preserved in miso and stew of beef tendon to Western items such as sweetfish confit and milt meunière. With such an array of appetisers, the sake slips down easily.	locatelliflorian	\N	\N
3339070648	Nikomi Kimura	6-18-6 Tanimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Izakaya	5.0	¥¥	f	t	Home cooking and sake, enfolded in the nostalgic charm of a Japanese-style house. ‘Nikomi’ means ‘stew’, and stews are the chef’s signature dishes. Sukiyaki-style simmered beef and tofu, stewed beef tendon with konnyaku, and chicken meatballs are offered year-round^ in winter, oden is added to the menu. ‘Fukusa Tamago’ is a rolled omelette mixed with vegetables. Western favourites such as macaroni salad and gratin can be had as well. The dishes with which the chef displays her talents are simple and full of warmth.	tohnjohn	\N	\N
3337235838	Sakeya Sakana Yoshimura	1-13-21 Tenjimbashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining,Notable sake list		Nessun premio	Izakaya	5.0	¥¥	t	t	This kappo izakaya is run by a sake-loving couple. The husband tends the kitchen, the wife warms the sake. The menu, crafted to double as bar snacks, is dominated by seafood. Jellied conger eel broth, vegetables seasoned with vinegar and miso and freshly deep-fried tofu are standard fare. Attention is paid to details: seaweed is toasted over charcoal, dried bonito shaved just before serving. The intimate pub seats three guests at the most^ on certain days, it’s exclusively for a single tippler.	battagliadaniela	\N	\N
3339517520	Shokudo Akari	2F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Izakaya	4.0	¥¥	f	f	Akari shines out of a detached house in the entertainment district. The nest of criss-crossing wooden poles hung from the ceiling represent the handheld pine torches of the Oto Fire Festival in Wakayama, as well as the flame of the chef’s passion for cooking. Expect everything from familiar favourites such as gingered chicken liver, potato salad and deep-fried horse mackerel, to seasonal flavours. Naturally, this true son of Wakayama takes pride in grilling with Kishu Bincho charcoal. Every February 6, the chef returns to his native prefecture to take part in the Fire Festival.	novakmonica	\N	\N
3333076273	Suigyo Murabayashi	B1F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Izakaya	4.0	¥¥	t	t	Since his apprentice days, the chef has made a habit of touring Japan to build trust with fishermen. The blessing that flows from that effort is the high-quality seafood delivered to him from each region. The first dish offered is sashimi in a large bowl. Fish of the season arrives little by little, in wondrous variety, as the sake flows nicely. Fish stew is prepared in soy sauce and water only, bringing out the natural sweetness of the flesh. The remaining broth is served with fresh-cooked rice, wasting nothing.	popovchloe	\N	\N
3332553827	The New World	B2F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Wheelchair access	https://sio-yoyogiuehara.com/restaurants/the-new-world/	Nessun premio	Izakaya	5.0	¥	f	t	Its space glittering with pink neon signs and mirror balls, creates a whole new world for izakaya. In similar vein, the cuisine is genre-free: many imaginative offerings break free from the confines of Japanese gastronomy. Examples include a salad of canned mackerel and shiraae of fresh fruit in season with mascarpone. The whole restaurant abounds with originality, as the chef’s playful spirit puts smiles on patrons’ faces.	bigazzimattia	\N	\N
3333156242	Oryori Amenimomakezu	4-10-17 Nishitemma	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining,Notable sake list		Nessun premio	Japanese	3.0	¥¥	f	f	On a narrow alley off Oimatsu-dori, the backlit sign saying ‘Ame ni mo makezu’ (Be not Defeated by the Rain) arrests the eye. The script has a character all its own, as the owner-chef wrote it himself using chopsticks dipped in charcoal. Fare is prepared with plenty of fermented seasonings to complement the sake. Grilled fish is marinated in malted rice^ chicken liver garnished with rice-bran bread. The way of living shown in Miyazawa Kenji’s masterpiece is manifested in the food on offer.	alfieriigor	\N	\N
3334868983	kushiage 010	4F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Kushiage	4.0	¥¥	f	f	These creative kushiage incorporate culinary influences from many countries. The sheer variety is endlessly fun. For your first skewer, you might start with angel shrimp from New Caledonia. The samgyeopsal (grilled pork-belly slices) wrapped in perilla and Korean lettuce is spiced with gochujang for a real Korean treat. Karaage, marinated in a sauce of spices such as star anise and five-spice powder and sprinkled with tapioca flour, fills the air with the fragrance of Taiwan.	cernyfrida	\N	\N
3332253502	Kushikatsu Gojoya	2-1-11 Uchihiranomachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining	https://94katu5jyoya.com/	Nessun premio	Kushiage	3.0	¥	f	f	The tempo in the kitchen is lively as cooks deep-fry these imaginative skewers. Order the omakase, which only ends when your belly is full. An amazing 25 types of skewers are normally available—you’ll want to see them all. Korean kimchi, Indian curry, Italian tomato sauce and so on take you on a journey from country to country. A fusion of Osaka’s fried foods with traditional cuisines of the world.	valentimatteo	\N	\N
3332221200	Chukasoba Mugen	5-5-10 Ebie	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	5.0	¥	t	t	While eating ramen, salt water from the noodles gradually dilutes the soup. You can experience this for yourself. First, pour a little of the soup into a separate bowl. Finish the noodles in the original bowl, then try sipping the reserved soup—the difference in taste will amaze you. The harmony created by a bowl of ramen goes beyond taste. The slurping of noodles forms a duet with the classical music in the background. The joy of ramen knows no bounds: ‘mugen’.	salajakub	\N	\N
3334023420	Chukasoba Uemachi	A-22 Uemachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	4.0	¥	t	f	The first thing you notice at Uemachi are the constant queues outside the door. The shop took the name of the neighbourhood as it aims to be a place beloved by locals. Its signature ramen dishes are ‘Shoyu’ (soy sauce) and ‘Shio’ (salt). The broth derives its richness from its meat base^ the flavour of dried fermented fish and crunchy sardines add depth. Insisting on noodles with a satisfying finish, the shop kneads its own. The fare is somehow nostalgic, in both taste and presentation.	zaitsevvictoria	\N	\N
3333387384	Hommachi Seimenjo Chukasobakobo	B2F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	5.0	¥	f	t	When you visit this ramen shop launched by a dried foods wholesaler, take note of the soup, which epitomises the brilliance of the dashi soup stock. Its well-rounded fragrance derives from multiple types of dried fish flakes. Kombu kelp, pike conger and chicken bones each add layers of flavour, creating body and finish. Heightening the taste are two types of noodles made on the premises: flat noodles to soak up the soup, round ones for their texture.	coppolaartem	\N	\N
3335350021	Kamigata Rainbow	4-6-3 Katsuyama	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	5.0	¥	f	t	A rainbow-coloured awning marks Kamigata Rainbow, a shop that pursues individuality even in ramen. The type of noodle differs for each type of ramen, creating unity in each. Soy sauce is redolent with lashings of small, dried sardines. In the curry ramen, curry paste floating in the soup changes delightfully as it melts. The menu includes items limited to each season, tickling one’s curiosity.	smirnovjessica	\N	\N
3335638366	Mugito Mensuke	3-4-12 Toyosaki	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	4.0	¥	f	t	Pursuing an original approach to ramen, the chef works with carefully selected ingredients.  Soup is prepared from free-range chicken and guinea fowl to bring out maximum flavour. Noodles, produced on the premises, are crafted for a delicious finish. Wontons stuffed with wagyu filling are the very flavour of luxury. Counter seating features an earthen wall made by a plasterer and wooden trays typical of Japanese restaurants. A ceaseless drive for improvement is evident in every bowl of ramen.	tudoroh	\N	\N
3336943658	Ramen Hayato	1-2-2 Minamimorimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	5.0	¥	f	f	This long-serving ramen shop is loved by Japanese people for its “Three Great Ramens”: soy-sauce, salt and miso. First-time visitors are recommended to order the soy-sauce ramen, but once you learn that the Hokkaido-born chef studied in Sapporo, you might be tempted to go for the miso ramen. The soy-sauce ramen is served in a double soup of meat-based stock and seafood-based stock. “Ura-Shoyu”, the ‘hidden soy-sauce ramen’ not listed on the menu, uses meat-based stock only. If you’re having trouble deciding, start with the ‘official’ menu.	morettipaolo	\N	\N
3336949199	Ramen Kuon	B2F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Ramen	3.0	¥	t	t	The chef takes pride in his “Shio Soba” (salt noodles), his quest for a bowl of perfection. Taste the seaweed—sea lettuce and nori—and you can feel the rustle of sea breezes. Triple soup of fish, shellfish and chicken is prepared using Pi-Water, a water that is said to be extremely similar to water in living tissue. High hydration ramen noodles, prepared with a blend of three types of wheat flour, produces a texture similar to noodles kneaded by hand. Three types of chashu are served as well, combining pork and chicken. Bringing out the best in soup, noodles and chashu for a triple treat.	fischerbogdan	\N	\N
3337851344	Ayamedo	2-2-26 Andojimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only		Nessun premio	Soba	3.0	¥	t	f	The building used to be a merchant house and now sports a Showa-era vibe. Side dishes such as tofu preserved in miso, buckwheat dumplings and bowls of duck meat fill the menu to accompany drinks. Not a few guests can be seen tipping a cup or two from noon onwards in the retro atmosphere. The soba noodles are generously piled high like a mountain on a wicker draining basket, dazzling the eyes, and each noodle, with its sharp, angular shape, offers a firm yet smooth texture.	palombasauro	\N	\N
3335532967	Naniwa Okina	4-1-18 Nishitemma	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning	https://naniwa-okina.co.jp/	Nessun premio	Soba	5.0	¥	f	t	The third-generation owner-chef decided to switch from machine-rolled to hand-rolled soba, so he learned the technique from a master. After thoroughly straining the soba, but without over-cooling it, he cuts the noodles neither thin nor thick, but just right. Dipping sauce is medium-salty to bring out the aroma of the buckwheat seeds, and the dish is arranged beautifully. A full range of snacks are on offer. We hope Japan will always have soba shops like this one.	ferraroedoardo	\N	\N
3337375942	Shitennoji Hayauchi	1-12-16 Shitennoji	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://shitennoji-hayauchi.com/	Nessun premio	Soba	3.0	¥	t	t	Oribe stoneware and distinctive Japanese-style paintings give presence to the interior. Crockery is produced by a potter with whom the owner-chef has cultivated a relationship. Recycled materials and earthen walls give the space a rough-hewn feel and a folk-art charm. Two kinds of soba are prepared: fine-ground seiro soba and coarse-ground inaka soba. Both are rolled to the same thickness. At lunchtime, customers start queuing in front of this popular shop before it opens. ‘Soba trays’ of soba with snacks are available with reservations.	polakcristina	\N	\N
3338318886	Soba Takama	7-12-14 Tenjimbashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥	t	t	‘Mori soba’ means ‘soba piled high’, and it is. Soba is served on seiro (bamboo or wicker baskets) because in the days of the Tokugawa shoguns it was common to steam the noodles. Piling soba into a mountain is another practice rooted in history. Made from buckwheat grains stripped of their black husks, this mori soba has a delicious finish. Inaka soba, made from husked whole buckwheat grains, is gloriously fragrant. Dipping sauce brightened with kombu kelp reflects the dashi stock culture of the Kansai region. We love the generous portions—one plate fully satisfies.	locatelliflorian	\N	\N
3331666334	Sobadokoro Toki	1-3-4 Dojima	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning	https://www.soba-toki.com/	Nessun premio	Soba	5.0	¥	f	f	Over the front door of this shop in Kitashinchi hangs a shimenawa, a twisted straw rope that wards off evil. Inscribed on a wooden block over the shimenawa is the phrase ‘Senkyaku Banrai’: ‘May customers arrive in the thousands and tens of thousands!’ In hopes the wish will come true, the doors are kept open until late at night. The sprawling menu ranges from appetizers that accompany sake to seasonal soba dishes. At lunchtime, soba accompanied by sushi is a nice surprise. In summer, chilled curry soba is brightly festooned with veggies. In winter, oyster soba in white miso soup warms the body from the inside. The special preparations for each season give something extra to look forward to.	lehmannlucian	\N	\N
3332809115	Sobakiri Arabompu	4-1-11 Nishitemma	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥	t	t	‘Arabompu’ was a favourite word of Edo-era haiku poet Kobayashi Issa. It means ‘living a free and ordinary life’. For the chef here, it means approaching his subjects, buckwheat and soba, from a natural stance. To watch his series of tasks—parboiling, washing, then plating the soba noodles—there’s no substitute for a seat at the counter. The ‘Soba Zanmai’ set offers three types of soba—nihachi, juwari, and arabiki—served as half bowls of each for tasting. Kanto-style dipping sauce recalls the days of the chef’s apprenticeship.	rusnakhannah	\N	\N
3334168249	Sobakiri Gaku	7-1-54 Tanimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥	f	t	It’s counter seating only in this modern interior accented with natural materials. The chef likes his soba to have a certain translucency, so he kneads it into ni-hachi, which contains 20% wheat flour. Soba is served cold on wicker baskets, not in soup, so patrons can appreciate the fragrance and firmness of the grain. ‘Kaeshi’, a mixture of soy sauce, sugar and mirin, is used to good effect in the rich dipping sauce, displaying the chef’s Kanto training. ‘Gaku’ means ‘mountain and sky’, signifying the chef’s commitment to attain ever higher goals.	colombostefano	\N	\N
3337705491	Sobakiri Imose	2-9-2 Tamatsukuri	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥	t	f	‘Imose’ refers to Mt. Imose in Wakayama Prefecture in the Manyoshu. The owner-chef chose it in tribute to his birthplace. This charming space with its sunken hearth recalls the rural scenes of his youth. Eager for customers to taste the differences in texture and aroma of soba types, he produces it in the nihachi, inaka and juwari styles. The kakinohazushi, sushi of shimesaba wrapped in a persimmon leaf, is hometown cooking in Wakayama ^ order it with soba for a diverting pairing.	cervigaia	\N	\N
3331406547	Sobakiri Karani	2-11-26 Sagisu	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only		Nessun premio	Soba	4.0	¥	f	f	Sobakiri Karani worked with a woodworking artist to create the interior using re-used wooden materials. Large tables with mismatched chairs and unique ceiling paintings express a whimsical spirit. The menu’s appetisers, including tofu pickled in unrefined sake and duck-and-onion miso, tempts one to a cup of sake. Soba is coarse-ground and cut into both thin and thick noodles, furnishing variety in finish and flavours. Enjoy soba the way you like it in a congenial atmosphere.	alfieriigor	\N	\N
3339334956	Donostia	1F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://donostia.site	Nessun premio	Spanish	5.0	¥¥	f	t	Donostia is the Basque name for Spain’s gourmet capital of San Sebastián. Pinchos and tortillas line the glass case on the counter, reinforcing the informal vibe of a Spanish tapas bar. Dry-cured ham, marinated sardines, squid frit and other delights are served a little at a time on small dishes. Seafood salsa verde redolent with herbs and bean-and-giblet stew are standard fare. Taste country cooking rich with the distinctiveness of the Basque countryside.	benedettifilip	\N	\N
3330947605	EL ALMA	8-19-12 Fukushima	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.elalma-fukushima.com/	Nessun premio	Spanish	4.0	¥¥	t	f	El Alma feels like a culinary journey, as the dishes hail from every region of Spain. The shrimp-and-mushroom ajillo represents the traditional flavours of southern Spain. Salted cod and almond sauce lend the tapas salad a Catalan flair. The meat dishes are charcoal-grilled for a smoky flavour that evokes the Basque Country. The chef, who’s made numerous trips to Spain, aims for an authentic feel that conveys the richness of regional Spanish cuisine.	dumitresculara	\N	\N
3339676561	gastroteka bimendi	1-5-9 Utsubohommachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://www.etxola.com/	Nessun premio	Spanish	4.0	¥¥	f	f	This bar offers pinchos and tapas, or Spanish cuisine served on small plates. A casual place to enjoy Basque home cooking along with drinks from Spain. The Basque Country is rich with the blessings of mountains and sea, so the range of offerings covers a wide gamut. Each item is simple, the portions generous. Though a continent apart, Spain and Japan share a love of honest cooking in which natural flavours come to the fore.	lehmannlucian	\N	\N
3339149373	AKA to SHIRO	3F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥	t	f	The chef uses two types of vinegared rice: red-vinegared rice for tuna and blue-backed fish, and white-vinegared rice for shrimp and white-fleshed fish. The red vinegar is a style popular in the Kanto region, while the ‘white’ vinegar (rice vinegar with sugar) is a Kansai speciality with strong roots in oshizushi. The combination of red and white, east and west, creates a dialogue between variations, respecting the spirit of each. Aka to Shiro also means ‘red and white’.	smirnovjessica	\N	\N
3331686744	Sakamoto Sushi	1-23-13 Nishikujo	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥	f	f	With Nishikujo Station to guide them to the spot, every evening customers gather at Sakamoto Sushi, where the familiar chef greets them with a smile. The omakase menu, which begins with a snack, is astounding in variety and breadth. Fish are painstakingly prepared by methods such as marination and searing, and are paired with white sushi rice in the Kansai way. Nigirizushi are interspersed with bozushi and hand-rolled sushi to keep things interesting. The chef uses the highest-grade fish, yet the prices won’t break the bank.	georgesculalla	\N	\N
3337431891	Sushidokoro Kaihara	5-4 Ikedacho	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Sushi	4.0	¥¥	t	t	Omakase from a veteran of Japanese cuisine. The meal begins with grated vegetables and appetizers. Nigiri emphasises the toppings, with fish cut thick and sushi rice more modest in quantity. Smaller sushi pieces enable guests to sample a variety of fish to their hearts’ content. An ingenious twist is that the soy sauce varies by topping: shrimp is served with egg yolk steeped in soy sauce, fatty tuna with foamy soy sauce, and so on. This self-taught sushi artisan never stops learning and rewarding guests with the results of his labours.	frolovjulia	\N	\N
3337042957	Sushitsune	2-4-3 Tenjimbashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://sushitsune.jp/	Nessun premio	Sushi	3.0	¥¥	f	f	The dish of renown here is the bateira, or pressed mackerel sushi, conceived by Sushitsune’s first-generation owner. The fourth-generation chef continues this tradition, now popular throughout Osaka, in which pickled mackerel is pressed onto sushi rice in a boat-shaped wooden frame and dressed with white-sheet kombu. With deft handling, skilfully applying just the right force to unravel the sushi rice, the chef displays the fine skill of long practice. Bozushi, of horse mackerel and conger eel, and temarizushi are exemplars of Osaka food culture. This is the place to sample true Naniwa oshizushi, beloved by ‘Japan’s Kitchen’ of Osaka.	gallofilippo	\N	\N
3332984754	Tempura Kozaki	4-11-38 Matsuzakicho	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tempura	3.0	¥¥	f	t	Tempura Kozaki expresses the seasons through ten pieces of tempura. The procession begins with tiger prawn, then alternates between vegetables and seafood. Experience in other disciplines of Japanese cuisine shines in the choice of ingredients such as deboned pike conger and Japanese mugwort gluten cake. Pieces are lightly deep-fried in cottonseed oil and served with warm tentsuyu. Frequent changes of coating show the skill of the seasoned veteran. This popular restaurant is full night after night^ for the first Sunday of each month, reservations are accepted three months in advance.	benedettifilip	\N	\N
3337895324	JIBUNDOKI	4-5-11 Minamikyuhojimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Teppanyaki, Okonomiyaki	3.0	¥¥	t	f	The owner-chef pours his creativity into teppanyaki dishes of incredible variety. In true Osaka style, both pork and mixed okonomiyaki are available. Though the batter is thick, the finished texture is light. Imaginative grilled skewers hint at the cuisines of many countries, with offerings such as nama-fu, salmon tartare and tteokbokki. All items are served in bite-sized pieces, so patrons can enjoy sampling a variety of flavours.	serrakevin	\N	\N
3339753930	Oribe	1-6-18 Shimmachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining	https://o-ri-be.com/	Nessun premio	Teppanyaki, Okonomiyaki	5.0	¥¥	f	t	French bistro and teppanyaki cooking are the draw at Oribe. The impressive range of appetisers includes items inspired by French standards such as paté and smoked salmon. Its famous cabbage steak was dreamed up from the essential ingredients of okonomiyaki. Speaking of okonomiyaki, the meal ends with Osaka’s famous savoury pancake, a popular item here. The restaurant was named for the chef’s penchant for Oribe ware, and this style of pottery inspires the green theme for its décor.	farinalucas	\N	\N
3333619993	tanpopo	6F	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Teppanyaki, Okonomiyaki	3.0	¥¥	t	f	The chef has continually upped his culinary game through a quarter-century of self-instruction, hopping from restaurant to restaurant to sharpen his instincts. The result is his cooking with iron and copper plates. Fluffy omelettes and deep-fried knots of fish paste are grilled on copper, pungent stir-fried noodles and beef steaks on iron. Tonpeiyaki (cabbage and pork wrapped in an omelette) is braced with sage for a hint of Italian. Rum is added to crêpes in the French style. Imagination and passion are cooked into every dish.	basileryan	\N	\N
3335742368	Tonkatsu Daiki	1-6-2 Higashishinsaibashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tonkatsu	5.0	¥	t	f	‘Daiki’ was the full name when the owner-chef’s parents ran it as a teishoku restaurant. The second-generation owner-chef switched to specialising in tonkatsu. Pork from Kagoshima is deep-fried in salad oil to provide the batter with just the right crispness. His experience in Chinese cooking comes to the fore in the piquantly spiced mustard sauce He pays special attention to the rice, too, cooking it in small batches.	montanarijack	\N	\N
3333780371	Tonkatsu Fujii	1-11-5 Sembayashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Tonkatsu	3.0	¥	t	f	The chef came over from the world of French cuisine to fry tonkatsu. Ingredients and cooking techniques are a complex mix of various elements, creating flavours found nowhere else. Wielding a blend of multiple oils, the chef lends his skills as a baker to the coating. Red wine accentuates the sauce. Experience inspires the ideas of this chef, who was indelibly impressed by the tonkatsu craftsmen of Tokyo. A chance meeting that changed a life, resulting in beautiful flavours.	amicowilliam	\N	\N
3337186588	Tonkatsu KATSU Hana	2-2-11 Kitakyuhojimachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining	https://katsuhana.jp/	Nessun premio	Tonkatsu	3.0	¥	f	f	Only name-brand pork from various regions is used so patrons can learn what truly delicious pork tastes like. The chef is always on the lookout for new pork brands, allowing diners to sample different flavours in tonkatsu every time they visit. If undecided between the tenderloin and the pork loin, the combination platter is the way to go. The restaurant is named ‘Hana’ because that is the name of the chef’s wife. Their warm and affable personalities add to the appeal, heightening satisfaction with the fare.	giulianijames	\N	\N
3330746120	Aozora blue	4-5-8 Hiranomachi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning	https://www.aozora.blue/	Nessun premio	Udon	4.0	¥	f	f	This distinctive udon is prepared using the techniques of handmade soba, which the chef mastered before switching to udon. Unpolished wheat is ground on a millstone. The experience of the soba craftsman lives in the principle of ‘freshly ground, freshly rolled, freshly boiled’. We recommend the Arabiki Zaru Udon (coarse-ground udon served on a wicker basket). Salt and wasabi accompany the dish, and the meal ends with udon water (not soba water, as is the practice elsewhere). Alcoholic drinks and snacks are selected to befit an udon meal. The chef’s discovery of wheat changed his life^ the paths of both soba and udon are long and narrow.	k�nigsara	\N	\N
3334654565	Ogimachi Udonya Asuro	3-8-3 Tenjimbashi	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Udon	4.0	¥	f	f	Located close to Tenjinbashi-suji, Kansai’s longest shopping street. The shop seems to be in competition for the longest queue to get in. The dish of renown is ‘Toriten to Taichikuwaten to Ontama Bukkake’ (udon topped with chicken tempura, sea bream paste cake tempura and soft-boiled egg). If the pure flavour of dipping sauce and noodles is what you’re after, try the ‘Hiyakake’. The clear sauce and delicious noodles are standouts, so the flavour of the seafood broth and the springiness of the noodles have your undivided attention. Choosing your favourite toppings adds to the fun.	zaitsevvictoria	\N	\N
3336636363	Oudon Yomogi	2-10-6 Sagisu	Japan	Osaka	Air conditioning,Cash only - lunch,Counter dining		Nessun premio	Udon	4.0	¥¥	t	t	This à la carte udon shop is a project of the proprietress of the Japanese restaurant Miyamoto. The use of bamboo gives the restaurant a kappo feel. In the evening the style is a fixed menu to which drinking snacks can be added. Great care is taken with the use of crockery – this is an udon restaurant with the flair of a soba place. The star of the udon items is the dashi soup stock, which is created with the basic soup stock, particularly drawing inspiration from soup dishes in traditional multi-course meals. A flavoursome lesson in Osaka dashi culture.	romaniriccardo	\N	\N
