3330858752	Muthu's Curry	#01-01	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Terrace,Wheelchair access	http://www.muthuscurry.com	Nessun premio	Indian	5.0	$$	f	f	‘Muthu’ means pearl in Tamil, which befits this gem (with another branche) serving traditional dishes from both the Southern and Northern regions of India. Originally called Muthu Makkan, the owner expanded and renamed the restaurant in 1982. The signature fish head, South Indian-style, uses a secret blend of spices. Swimmer crabs, masala prawns, lamb rack and tandoori chicken are also big hits among diners. Ask about the monthly specials.	parisibenjamin	\N	\N
3339120976	Cumi Bali	50 Tras Street	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning	https://www.cumibali.com	Nessun premio	Indonesian	5.0	$$	f	f	Relocated to Tras Street in 2021, this friendly restaurant serves authentic Indonesian cooking in a room enhanced by artifacts and bamboo-styled furnishings. The signature whole grilled squid Cumi Bali – succulent and springy in texture – gives the restaurant its name. It is particularly delicious in their prized homemade sauce made with a myriad of natural spices. Nasi Kunyit is a rice dish jazzed up by 11 spices such as turmeric, cinnamon and pandan leaves.	m�llercristian	\N	\N
3333299557	Fico	1300 East Coast Parkway	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Car park,Garden or park,Wheelchair access	https://www.ficomasseria.com	Nessun premio	Italian	5.0	$$	f	f	This white house styled after a masseria in Puglia is sited within East Coast Park. The sprawling lawn, spectacular ocean views and sound of the waves all exude a soothing vibe. The Pugliese chef is keen to bring authentic cuisine from his homeland to Singapore, of which focaccina and pasta made from scratch are the highlights. Try his San Marzano tomato focaccina with mozzarella and fresh basil, bursting with intense flavours.	zaitsevvictoria	\N	\N
3338086919	Hjh Maimunah (Jalan Pisang)	11 & 15 Jalan Pisang	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Cash only,Wheelchair access	https://www.hjmaimunah.com/en_SG	Nessun premio	Malaysian	5.0	$	f	t	In a narrow street behind Masjid Sultan mosque is a simple Malaysian restaurant that’s always busy – and it’s easy to see why: the ingredients are fresh, the food is delicious and the prices are affordable. Around 70% of dishes are Malaysian and 30% Indonesian^ it’s mostly self-service and the dishes to go for include Sundanese grilled chicken, beef rendang, lemak siput and tahu telur. The upstairs room is slightly more comfortable.	benettisergey	\N	\N
3334552753	The Coconut Club (Beach Road)	269 Beach Road	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Counter dining,Terrace	http://www.thecoconutclub.sg	Nessun premio	Malaysian	3.0	$$	f	t	This whitewashed traditional shophouse evokes unmistakably tropical vibes – wicker back chairs, wallpaper with banana leaf prints and cheerful blue-and-white tiled flooring. On the menu, the coconut theme is also obvious – the nasi lemak set is the best seller, but other dishes deserve equal attention too, such as ayam goreng berempah (spiced fried chicken with crispy rempah crumbs), and sago pudding with pandan, palm sugar and coconut cream.	silvestritommy	\N	\N
3330389313	Zhup Zhup	#01-01	Singapore	Singapore			Nessun premio	Noodles	3.0	$	t	f	Formerly One Prawn & Co, Zhup Zhup still sees constant queues forming beneath its red awning. Book ahead to cut your wait time. The signature prawn noodles come in a set: a bowl of noodles plus rich prawn soup in a claypot. Out of the four types of noodles, the classic option is yellow noodles with thin bee hoon. The soup is loaded with flavours, imparted in particular by the tomalley of countless prawn heads.	schneidermihai	\N	\N
3332931901	Indocafé	35 Scotts Road	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Car park,Restaurant offering vegetarian menus	https://www.thehouseofindocafe.com/	Nessun premio	Peranakan	5.0	$$	t	t	Dark wood furniture and antiques add to the elegant colonial spirit of this restaurant nestled in a bungalow, also known as ‘The White House’. The menu offers Peranakan cuisine and the dishes look deceptively simple, but involve subtly blended spices and seasoning. Wagyu beef rendang uses the cheek cut for tenderness, while spices, fermented shrimp paste, chilli and coconut milk underscore the rich, deep flavour.	grimaldimorko	\N	\N
3333817174	The Blue Ginger (Tanjong Pagar)	97 Tanjong Pagar Road	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning	http://theblueginger.com	Nessun premio	Peranakan	5.0	$$	t	f	This is one of the most popular little gems in Chinatown and it’s easy to see why. The narrow house offers dining on three floors^ art by local artists add colour and character. In business for over 20 years, regulars keep returning for its aromatic cooking like Ngo Heong that delivers all the subtle and authentic flavours of Peranakan, as well as addictive sweets like Chendol. Better still, prices are as generous as the portions—that are great for sharing.	baroneandres	\N	\N
3337490792	True Blue Cuisine	47/49 Armenian Street	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Car park,Wheelchair access	https://truebluecuisine.com/	Nessun premio	Peranakan	5.0	$$	f	t	It’s less like walking into a restaurant, more like falling into the warm embrace of a much loved family member. Here it’s about nostalgia and paying homage to the food, history and customs of the Peranakans – you’ll find all the classic dishes, prepared in an authentic way, and the hard part is narrowing down your choice. The setting really is unique and the Ayam Buah Keluak is one of the signature.	puglieseadam	\N	\N
3332186253	Da Shi Jia Big Prawn Mee	89 Killiney Road	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Car park	https://dashijia.business.site	Nessun premio	Singaporean	3.0	$	f	t	Guests are spoiled for choices here. First, pick between prawn noodle soup and fried prawn bee hoon. For prawn mee, specify the size of these wild-caught marine crustaceans – from regular to XXL. Then pick your noodles –the yellow variety, kway teow, bee hoon, or any combination of those. For real big eaters, choose extra toppings of fish cake, pork ribs or sliced pork. Don’t forget about snacks like tofu skin prawn rolls and fried prawn balls on the side.	bassioleg	\N	\N
3337422434	Kok Sen	2/4 Keong Saik Road	Singapore	Singapore	Cash only		Nessun premio	Singaporean	3.0	$	f	f	In business for over 50 years, Kok Sen moved to another spot on the same street in 2022. Seats may be a bit cramped, but the new space has a nice feel to it. Round tables and green booth seats work well with a grey colour scheme and pale wood. Signature cze char classics are listed in colourful lightboxes – the prawn paste chicken is simply not to be missed^ fish cooked in various ways is also popular. Seasonal dishes may need to be pre-ordered.	gavrilovnora	\N	\N
3334589601	Sik Bao Sin (Desmond's Creation)	592 Geylang Road	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning,Cash only,Wheelchair access		Nessun premio	Singaporean	4.0	$	t	f	The second-generation owner-chef makes every dish according to traditional recipes for guaranteed authenticity. The concise menu only features 13 items, but seasonal dishes, not on the menu, are also available. The chef’s signature dish, sweet and sour pork, is made to order, so expect to wait. The ginger chicken is simply divine – tender chicken glazed in a mild chilli sauce alongside zesty pickled ginger shoots. Also, try the kale with prawns.	rossettijonas	\N	\N
3339377286	Song Fa Bak Kut Teh (New Bridge Road)	#01-01	Singapore	Singapore	Air conditioning	https://songfa.com.sg	Nessun premio	Singaporean	4.0	$	f	t	Much has changed since Mr Yeo started selling bak kut teh from his pushcart in Johor Road back in the late ’60s – nowadays it’s the second generation of the family who are running the show. They are still selling their celebrated pork ribs and peppery broth but are now doing so from 10+ shops – and this one, the simplest, seems to be the best. Queue, order, pay, and then tuck into your soup – when you’ve finished they’ll top it up for you.	stepanekgrace	\N	\N
